import { nativeOlympiadContent, p, h, sub, fig } from "./native-content";

// Source PDFs and crop provenance: docs/engineering/native-archive-batch-7-*.json.

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2004-t3"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Atomic Probe Microscope"),
    p("An atomic probe microscope measures the vertical displacement \\(z\\) of a cantilever by reflecting a laser onto a photodetector. The cantilever obeys \\(m\\ddot z+b\\dot z+kz=F\\), with \\(k=m\\omega_0^2\\) and weak damping \\(0<b/m\\ll\\omega_0\\). A piezoelectric tube supplies F. A lock-in amplifier multiplies its input by the reference \\(V_R=V_{R0}\\sin\\omega t\\), then retains only the dc component. A phase shifter and photodetector complete the measurement circuit as shown. Throughout, the source calls \\(\\omega=\\omega_0\\) resonance, appropriate in the stated weak-damping limit."),
    fig("batch7-probe-apparatus", "Original atomic probe microscope, photodetector, phase shifter, piezotube and lock-in circuit with labelled reference signals."),
    sub("a.amplitude. Displacement amplitude"),
    p("For \\(F=F_0\\sin\\omega t\\), write \\(z=A\\sin(\\omega t-\\phi)\\), with \\(A>0\\), \\(0\\le\\phi\\le\\pi\\). Find A."),
    sub("a.phase. Phase relation"),
    p("Find \\(\\tan\\phi\\), state the phase quadrant, and give A and phi at \\(\\omega=\\omega_0\\)."),
    sub("b.frequency. Lock-in frequency condition"),
    p("The input is \\(V_i=V_{i0}\\sin(\\omega_it-\\phi_i)\\), with all listed constants positive. What frequency condition is necessary for a nonzero dc output? State the additional phase condition."),
    sub("b.output. Lock-in output magnitude"),
    p("At the matching frequency, find the magnitude of the dc output."),
    sub("c. Phase-shifted microscope signal"),
    p("The phase shifter changes the reference to \\(V_R^{\\prime}=V_{R0}\\sin(\\omega t+\\pi/2)\\). The piezotube produces \\(F=c_1V_R^{\\prime}\\), and the detector produces \\(V_i=c_2z\\). Find the dc output magnitude at \\(\\omega_0\\), taking \\(c_1,c_2>0\\)."),
    sub("d. Detectable mass change"),
    p("A small mass change shifts natural frequency by \\(\\Delta\\omega_0\\), producing phase change \\(\\Delta\\phi=\\pi/1800\\) at the original drive frequency. Find the mass change for \\(m=1.0\\times10^{-12}\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\), \\(k=1.0\\,\\mathrm{N/m}\\), \\(b/m=1.0\\times10^3\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}\\). You may use first-order binomial and tangent expansions."),
    sub("e. Force-gradient frequency"),
    p("A sample exerts additional force \\(f(h)\\), where h is tip-sample separation. Near equilibrium \\(h_0\\), write \\(f(h)=f(h_0)+c_3(h-h_0)\\). Find the new natural frequency."),
    sub("f.formula. Trapped-charge depth"),
    p("A tip with charge \\(Q=6e\\) passes over a trapped electron of charge \\(q=e\\), where \\(e=-1.6\\times10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm C\\). The maximum small positive shift \\(\\Delta\\omega_0\\) occurs directly over the electron. Find tip-electron distance \\(d_0\\), ignoring polarization and using Coulomb constant \\(k_e\\)."),
    sub("f.value. Charge depth value"),
    p("Evaluate the distance in nm for \\(\\Delta\\omega_0=20\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}\\), \\(m=10^{-12}\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\), \\(k=1.0\\,\\mathrm{N/m}\\), \\(k_e=9.0\\times10^9\\,\\mathrm{N\\,m^2/C^2}\\)."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Atomic Probe Microscope"),
    sub("a.amplitude. Displacement amplitude"),
    p("Equating sine and cosine coefficients after substitution gives \\[A=\\frac{F_0}{\\sqrt{m^2(\\omega_0^2-\\omega^2)^2+b^2\\omega^2}}.\\]"),
    sub("a.phase. Phase relation"),
    p("\\[\\tan\\phi=\\frac{b\\omega}{m(\\omega_0^2-\\omega^2)}.\\] The phase is below \\(\\pi/2\\) for \\(\\omega<\\omega_0\\), and above it for \\(\\omega>\\omega_0\\). At \\(\\omega_0\\), \\(A=F_0/(b\\omega_0)\\) and \\(\\phi=\\pi/2\\). The exact amplitude maximum is at \\(\\sqrt{\\omega_0^2-b^2/(2m^2)}\\), whose weak-damping difference from \\(\\omega_0\\) is neglected in the source."),
    sub("b.frequency. Lock-in frequency condition"),
    p("Product-to-sum gives a difference-frequency term and a sum-frequency term. A dc term is possible only if \\(\\omega=\\omega_i\\). It is nonzero only when \\(\\cos\\phi_i\\ne0\\)."),
    sub("b.output. Lock-in output magnitude"),
    p("The signed dc component is \\(V_{i0}V_{R0}\\cos\\phi_i/2\\), so its magnitude is \\(V_{i0}V_{R0}|\\cos\\phi_i|/2\\). The absolute value is required when the question asks for magnitude."),
    sub("c. Phase-shifted microscope signal"),
    p("The cantilever lags its drive by \\(\\pi/2\\), cancelling the phase-shifter advance. Thus the detector signal is in phase with the original reference. Its amplitude is \\(c_1c_2V_{R0}/(b\\omega_0)\\), giving \\[V_{dc}=\\frac{c_1c_2V_{R0}^2}{2b\\omega_0}.\\]"),
    sub("d. Detectable mass change"),
    p("With fixed k, \\(\\Delta\\omega_0=-\\omega_0\\Delta m/(2m)\\). Linearizing the phase at fixed drive gives \\(\\Delta\\phi=-2m\\Delta\\omega_0/b\\). Thus \\[\\Delta m=(b/\\omega_0)\\Delta\\phi=1.7\\times10^{-18}\\,\\mathrm{kg}.\\] The rendered source gives \\(k=1.0\\,\\mathrm{N/m}\\), not the 0.1 produced by its faulty text extraction."),
    sub("e. Force-gradient frequency"),
    p("The constant force moves the equilibrium, while the gradient changes stiffness to \\(k-c_3\\). Thus \\[\\omega_0^{\\prime}=\\sqrt{\\omega_0^2-c_3/m},\\] assuming \\(k-c_3>0\\)."),
    sub("f.formula. Trapped-charge depth"),
    p("Directly over the charge, \\(f=k_eqQ/h^2\\), so \\(c_3=-2k_eqQ/d_0^3\\). To first order \\(\\Delta\\omega_0=-c_3/(2m\\omega_0)\\), giving \\[d_0=\\left(\\frac{k_eqQ}{m\\omega_0\\Delta\\omega_0}\\right)^{1/3}.\\]"),
    sub("f.value. Charge depth value"),
    p("\\(\\omega_0=10^6\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}\\), giving \\(d_0=41\\,\\mathrm{nm}\\)."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2007-t2"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Capacitive accelerometer"),
    p("A simplified automobile accelerometer uses a movable capacitor plate and springs. Ignore gravity as a force on the apparatus. All plates have area A, gaps are much smaller than plate dimensions, and air permittivity is \\(\\epsilon_0\\). For part 1 a movable plate on one spring k faces a fixed plate. Its unstretched separation is d and \\(C_0=\\epsilon_0A/d\\). The isolated plates carry fixed charges \\(\\pm Q\\). For parts 2 and 3 a mass M and a negligible-mass central plate are supported between two fixed plates by two springs, each of constant k. The fixed plates are held at \\(+V\\) and \\(-V\\). At zero acceleration each gap is d. Let \\(x>0\\) mean a central-plate displacement towards the \\(+V\\) electrode. In state alpha the central plate is grounded. In state beta it is connected to ground through an initially uncharged capacitor \\(C_S\\). During parts 2 and 3 assume equilibrium relative to the accelerating car."),
    fig("batch7-single-capacitor", "Original fixed and movable capacitor plates with spring stiffness k and initial gap d."),
    sub("1.1. Fixed-charge attraction"),
    p("Find the force magnitude between the two isolated capacitor plates."),
    sub("1.2. Equilibrium displacement"),
    p("Find the movable-plate displacement towards the fixed plate."),
    sub("1.3. Equilibrium voltage"),
    p("Find the potential difference after mechanical equilibrium."),
    sub("1.4. Capacitance ratio"),
    p("Find \\(C/C_0\\)."),
    sub("1.5. Stored energy"),
    p("Find total electrical plus spring energy at equilibrium."),
    sub("2.1. Grounded-plate capacitor charges"),
    fig("batch7-differential-capacitor", "Original central plate and mass M between two sprung fixed plates, voltage sources, switch and signal capacitor Cs."),
    p("In state alpha find the charge magnitudes on the \\(+V\\)-side and \\(-V\\)-side capacitors as functions of x."),
    sub("2.2. Net electrical force"),
    p("Find the signed electrical force towards the positive electrode."),
    sub("2.3. Small-displacement force"),
    p("Keep only terms linear in \\(x/d\\)."),
    sub("2.4. Effective stiffness"),
    p("Write the combined spring and electric force as \\(-k_{eff}x\\), and find \\(k_{eff}\\)."),
    sub("2.5. Acceleration and lag"),
    p("For positive lag x towards the positive electrode, find the magnitude of the car acceleration away from that electrode."),
    sub("3.1. Series-capacitor signal"),
    p("In state beta find the central-plate potential \\(V_S\\) relative to ground."),
    sub("3.2. Linear voltage signal"),
    p("Neglect \\(x^2\\) against \\(d^2\\) and simplify the preceding result."),
    sub("4.1. Negligible electrical correction"),
    p("Now abandon the equilibrium assumption for a sudden braking event. Use \\(d=1.0\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\), \\(A=2.5\\times10^{-2}\\,\\mathrm{m^2}\\), \\(k=4.2\\times10^3\\,\\mathrm{N/m}\\), \\(\\epsilon_0=8.85\\times10^{-12}\\,\\mathrm{SI}\\), \\(V=12\\,\\mathrm V\\), \\(M=0.15\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\), and ignore friction. Find electric-force magnitude divided by total spring-force magnitude."),
    sub("4.2. Maximum braking displacement"),
    p("A car initially at constant velocity suddenly acquires constant acceleration of magnitude a. Find maximum displacement of the movable plate."),
    sub("4.3. Threshold capacitor"),
    p("In state beta the air bag triggers at \\(V_S=0.15\\,\\mathrm V\\). Choose \\(C_S\\) so it does not trigger for constant braking acceleration below \\(g=9.8\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}\\), but reaches the threshold at g."),
    sub("4.4. Time until head contact"),
    p("Estimate head-to-steering-wheel distance, then estimate time to contact when the car decelerates at g but the head initially keeps its constant velocity."),
    sub("4.5. Activation time and comparison"),
    p("Find the trigger time at deceleration g and compare with the contact-time estimate. Assume the air bag opens instantaneously."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Capacitive accelerometer"),
    sub("1.1. Fixed-charge attraction"),
    p("Only the other plate's field acts on a given plate. Its field is \\(Q/(2\\epsilon_0A)\\), so \\(F_E=Q^2/(2\\epsilon_0A)\\), independent of gap."),
    sub("1.2. Equilibrium displacement"),
    p("Balance spring and electrical forces: \\(kx=F_E\\), hence \\(x=Q^2/(2\\epsilon_0Ak)\\)."),
    sub("1.3. Equilibrium voltage"),
    p("The final gap is \\(d-x\\). Thus \\[V=\\frac{Qd}{\\epsilon_0A}\\left(1-\\frac{Q^2}{2\\epsilon_0Akd}\\right).\\] This requires the gap to remain positive."),
    sub("1.4. Capacitance ratio"),
    p("\\(C=\\epsilon_0A/(d-x)\\), so \\[C/C_0=\\left(1-\\frac{Q^2}{2\\epsilon_0Akd}\\right)^{-1}.\\]"),
    sub("1.5. Stored energy"),
    p("\\(U=Q^2(d-x)/(2\\epsilon_0A)+kx^2/2\\). Substitution gives \\[U=\\frac{Q^2d}{2\\epsilon_0A}\\left(1-\\frac{Q^2}{4\\epsilon_0Akd}\\right).\\]"),
    sub("2.1. Grounded-plate capacitor charges"),
    p("The gaps are \\(d-x\\) and \\(d+x\\). Therefore \\(Q_+=\\epsilon_0AV/(d-x)\\), \\(Q_-=\\epsilon_0AV/(d+x)\\). The central faces carry opposite signs."),
    sub("2.2. Net electrical force"),
    p("The two attractions oppose each other: \\[F_E=\\frac{\\epsilon_0AV^2}{2}\\left[\\frac1{(d-x)^2}-\\frac1{(d+x)^2}\\right].\\]"),
    sub("2.3. Small-displacement force"),
    p("Binomial expansion gives \\(F_E\\simeq2\\epsilon_0AV^2x/d^3\\). The electrical force is destabilizing."),
    sub("2.4. Effective stiffness"),
    p("Both springs supply restoring force, totalling \\(-2kx\\). Hence \\[k_{eff}=2k-2\\epsilon_0AV^2/d^3.\\] Stable centring requires this coefficient to be positive."),
    sub("2.5. Acceleration and lag"),
    p("In the car frame, inertial force balances the restoring force: \\(Ma=k_{eff}x\\). Thus \\[a=\\frac{2x}{M}(k-\\epsilon_0AV^2/d^3).\\] The signed acceleration in the +x direction is minus this value. This states explicitly the coordinate convention behind the sign in the source."),
    sub("3.1. Series-capacitor signal"),
    p("The isolated central conductor and its connected capacitor plate have zero net charge. With \\(C_+=\\epsilon_0A/(d-x)\\), \\(C_-=\\epsilon_0A/(d+x)\\), charge balance gives \\(C_+(V_S-V)+C_-(V_S+V)+C_SV_S=0\\). Therefore \\[V_S=\\frac{2\\epsilon_0AVx}{C_S(d^2-x^2)+2\\epsilon_0Ad}.\\]"),
    sub("3.2. Linear voltage signal"),
    p("\\[V_S\\simeq\\frac{2\\epsilon_0AVx}{d^2C_S+2\\epsilon_0Ad}.\\]"),
    sub("4.1. Negligible electrical correction"),
    p("The ratio is \\(\\epsilon_0AV^2/(kd^3)=7.6\\times10^{-9}\\). Thus electric forces can be neglected in the transient mechanical motion. The same smallness holds in state beta."),
    sub("4.2. Maximum braking displacement"),
    p("In the accelerating frame the displacement obeys \\(M\\ddot x+2kx=Ma\\), starting with zero displacement and relative velocity. Thus \\[x(t)=\\frac{Ma}{2k}[1-\\cos(\\sqrt{2k/M}\\,t)],\\qquad x_{max}=Ma/k.\\]"),
    sub("4.3. Threshold capacitor"),
    p("At the first displacement maximum, \\(x_{max}=Mg/k\\). Combine with 3.2 to get \\[C_S=\\frac{2\\epsilon_0A}{d}\\left(\\frac{VMg}{kV_Sd}-1\\right)=8.0\\times10^{-11}\\,\\mathrm F.\\]"),
    sub("4.4. Time until head contact"),
    p("For an estimated gap \\(l\\), the initial relative velocity is zero and closing distance is \\(gt_1^2/2\\). Thus \\(t_1=\\sqrt{2l/g}\\). A plausible \\(l=0.4\\) to \\(1\\,\\mathrm m\\) gives \\(t_1\\simeq0.3\\) to \\(0.5\\,\\mathrm s\\). This estimate depends on the chosen gap and is self-review."),
    sub("4.5. Activation time and comparison"),
    p("The threshold is reached at the first displacement maximum, half a mechanical period: \\[t_2=\\pi\\sqrt{M/(2k)}=0.013\\,\\mathrm s.\\] It is much shorter than the estimated \\(0.3\\) to \\(0.5\\,\\mathrm s\\), so this idealized model triggers in time. It omits the real inflation time."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2008-t2"] = {
  statement: [
    h("CHERENKOV LIGHT AND RING IMAGING COUNTER"),
    p("A charged particle travels at \\(v=\\beta c>c/n\\) in a transparent medium of index n. Its Cherenkov light makes angle \\(\\theta=\\arccos[1/(\\beta n)]\\) with its trajectory. Keep only the requested small-angle terms in \\(\\theta\\) and mirror-axis tilt \\(\\alpha\\). In a ring-imaging Cherenkov counter, the beam crosses a concave spherical mirror at S. Its centre is C, focal length f, and the beam line IS makes small angle alpha with SC. Numerical momenta are in GeV/c and rest masses in GeV/\\(c^2\\)."),
    fig("batch7-cherenkov-cone", "Original particle path from A to B and rays making Cherenkov angle theta with the trajectory."),
    sub("1.1. Wavefront construction"),
    p("A particle passes A at time zero and B at time \\(t_1\\), emitting spherical waves along the path at speed \\(c/n\\). Draw the envelope at \\(t_1\\) in a plane containing AB."),
    sub("1.2. Wavefront cone angle"),
    p("Find the angle \\(\\varphi\\) between a side of this wavefront cone and the particle trajectory."),
    sub("2. Ring centre and radius"),
    p("Use a ray sketch to explain the ring image and find its centre O and radius."),
    sub("3.1. Threshold radiator pressures"),
    p("A beam with momentum 10.0 GeV/c contains protons, kaons and pions of masses 0.94, 0.50 and 0.14 GeV/\\(c^2\\). Air has \\(n=1+aP\\), \\(a=2.7\\times10^{-4}\\,\\mathrm{atm^{-1}}\\). Find the threshold pressures for each species."),
    sub("3.2.pressure. Half-radius kaon ring"),
    p("Find pressure \\(P_{1/2}\\) such that the kaon ring radius is half the pion ring radius."),
    sub("3.2.angles. Ring angles and proton visibility"),
    p("At that pressure find both angles, and determine whether protons make a ring."),
    sub("4.1. Momentum-to-angle width"),
    p("At \\(P_{1/2}\\), the momentum distribution is centred on 10 GeV/c with half width at half height \\(\\Delta p\\). Find \\(\\Delta\\theta_K/\\Delta p\\) and \\(\\Delta\\theta_\\pi/\\Delta p\\)."),
    sub("4.2. Resolvable momentum width"),
    p("Require \\(\\theta_\\pi-\\theta_K>10(\\Delta\\theta_K+\\Delta\\theta_\\pi)\\). Find the largest permitted momentum half width."),
    sub("5.1. Water radiation threshold"),
    p("For water with n = 1.33, find threshold kinetic energy for rest mass M."),
    sub("5.2. Radioactive particles"),
    p("Find threshold energies for alpha particles of mass 3.8 GeV/\\(c^2\\) and electrons of mass 0.51 MeV/\\(c^2\\). A radioactive source emits particles with at most a few MeV. Which species explains Cherenkov's observed water light?"),
    sub("6.1. Chromatic angular spread"),
    p("Visible wavelengths span 0.4 to 0.8 micrometres. Across this range n decreases linearly by 2 percent of \\(n-1\\). For 10 GeV/c pions in air at 6 atm, find the angular difference between the two ends of the visible spectrum."),
    sub("6.2.1. Two broadening mechanisms"),
    p("The pion momentum half width is now 0.3 GeV/c at a centre of 10 GeV/c. Estimate ring broadening from dispersion and from momentum spread, using half-width conventions."),
    sub("6.2.2. Ring colour ordering"),
    p("Describe colour from the inner edge to the outer edge."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("CHERENKOV LIGHT AND RING IMAGING COUNTER"),
    sub("1.1. Wavefront construction"),
    p("Every emitted sphere has radius \\((c/n)(t_1-t)\\), proportional to its centre's distance \\(v(t_1-t)\\) behind B. The common tangent envelope is a cone with vertex B. In the selected plane its two sides are straight tangent lines extending backwards from B. Draw the endpoint cap if restricting emission to the finite segment AB."),
    sub("1.2. Wavefront cone angle"),
    p("The right triangle between B, a wave centre and its tangent point gives \\(\\sin\\varphi=1/(\\beta n)\\). Thus \\(\\varphi=\\arcsin[1/(\\beta n)]=\\pi/2-\\theta\\)."),
    sub("2. Ring centre and radius"),
    p("Parallel Cherenkov rays at a given azimuth focus to one point of the focal plane. Their fixed angle theta around IS generates a circle. The line through C parallel to IS intersects that plane at O. If F is the axial focus, \\(FO=f\\alpha\\) to first order, and ring radius \\(r=f\\theta\\). The ray diagram must show these constructions."),
    sub("3.1. Threshold radiator pressures"),
    p("With \\(K=Mc/p\\), \\(\\beta=1/\\sqrt{1+K^2}\\). The exact threshold is \\(P_{min}=(\\sqrt{1+K^2}-1)/a\\), approximately \\(K^2/(2a)\\). This gives about 16 atm for protons, 4.6 atm for kaons and 0.36 atm for pions. Radiation requires pressure above the threshold."),
    sub("3.2.pressure. Half-radius kaon ring"),
    p("For small theta and small \\(n-1\\), \\(\\theta^2\\simeq2aP-K^2\\). Set \\(\\theta_\\pi=2\\theta_K\\), giving \\[aP_{1/2}=\\frac{4K_K^2-K_\\pi^2}{6}.\\] The pressure is about 6.0 atm."),
    sub("3.2.angles. Ring angles and proton visibility"),
    p("The angles are \\(\\theta_K\\simeq0.0277\\,\\mathrm{rad}=1.6^\\circ\\), \\(\\theta_\\pi\\simeq0.0554\\,\\mathrm{rad}=3.2^\\circ\\). Protons do not radiate because this pressure is below their approximately 16 atm threshold."),
    sub("4.1. Momentum-to-angle width"),
    p("Differentiate \\(\\cos\\theta=1/(n\\beta)\\) at fixed n. To leading order, \\[\\frac{d\\theta}{dp}\\simeq\\frac{K^2}{\\theta p}.\\] The values are about \\(0.51^\\circ/(\\mathrm{GeV}/c)\\) for kaons and \\(0.020^\\circ/(\\mathrm{GeV}/c)\\) for pions."),
    sub("4.2. Resolvable momentum width"),
    p("The angular separation is about \\(1.6^\\circ\\) and the sum-width coefficient is \\(0.53^\\circ/(\\mathrm{GeV}/c)\\). Therefore \\(\\Delta p<1.6/(10\\times0.53)\\simeq0.30\\,\\mathrm{GeV}/c\\)."),
    sub("5.1. Water radiation threshold"),
    p("At threshold \\(\\beta=1/n\\). Relativistic kinetic energy gives \\[T_{min}=Mc^2\\left[\\frac1{\\sqrt{1-1/n^2}}-1\\right]\\simeq0.517Mc^2.\\]"),
    sub("5.2. Radioactive particles"),
    p("Thresholds are \\(1.96\\,\\mathrm{GeV}\\) for alpha particles and \\(0.264\\,\\mathrm{MeV}\\) for electrons. Only electrons can exceed their threshold at the given radioactive energies."),
    sub("6.1. Chromatic angular spread"),
    p("\\(\\delta n=0.02(n-1)\\), and differentiating the Cherenkov relation gives \\(\\delta\\theta=\\delta n/(n\\tan\\theta)\\simeq\\delta n/\\theta\\). With \\(n=1.00162\\), \\(\\theta\\simeq0.055\\,\\mathrm{rad}\\), this is \\(0.033^\\circ\\)."),
    sub("6.2.1. Two broadening mechanisms"),
    p("The full visible angular spread from 6.1 corresponds to an approximate dispersion half width \\(0.017^\\circ\\). Momentum spread contributes \\(0.020\\times0.3=0.006^\\circ\\). Dispersion is about three times larger. The source treats the spectral half-span as a half-width estimate, not a specified exact spectral line shape."),
    sub("6.2.2. Ring colour ordering"),
    p("Long-wavelength red light has lower n and smaller theta. Short-wavelength blue/violet light has larger theta. The ordering is red, then a white overlap region, then blue/violet at the outside."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2009-t2"] = {
  statement: [
    h("DOPPLER LASER COOLING AND OPTICAL MOLASSES"),
    p("A two-level atom of mass m moves along +x with speed v. Ground-state internal energy is zero and excited-state energy is \\(\\hbar\\omega_0\\). A laser photon travels along -x with laboratory frequency \\(\\omega_L\\), energy \\(\\hbar\\omega_L\\), and momentum \\(-\\hbar q\\), where \\(q=\\omega_L/c>0\\). Assume \\(v/c\\ll1\\) and \\(\\hbar q/(mv)\\ll1\\). Keep first-order corrections appropriate to the observable and discard their products. Absorption is followed by spontaneous emission with equal probability along either x direction. Part I uses a resonant incident beam. All atomic energies exclude the constant rest energy."),
    fig("batch7-cooling-absorption", "Original moving two-level atom, incident counterpropagating photon and the laboratory x axis."),
    sub("1a. Absorption resonance"),
    p("Write the resonance condition for the counterpropagating photon."),
    sub("1b. Momentum after absorption"),
    p("Find the atomic laboratory momentum immediately after absorption."),
    sub("1c. Energy after absorption"),
    p("Find total atomic kinetic plus internal energy after absorption."),
    sub("2a. Backward photon energy"),
    p("If the spontaneous photon is emitted along -x, find its laboratory energy."),
    sub("2b. Backward photon momentum"),
    p("Find that emitted photon's signed laboratory momentum."),
    sub("2c. Atom after backward emission"),
    p("Find the final atom momentum for this emission direction."),
    sub("2d. Atom energy after backward emission"),
    p("Find its final energy after backward emission."),
    sub("3a. Forward photon energy"),
    p("If emission is along +x, find the photon's laboratory energy."),
    sub("3b. Forward photon momentum"),
    p("Find the forward photon momentum, retaining its first-order Doppler correction."),
    sub("3c. Atom after forward emission"),
    p("Find the final atomic momentum at the stipulated atomic-momentum order."),
    sub("3d. Atom energy after forward emission"),
    p("Find the final atomic energy consistently with momentum and energy conservation."),
    sub("4a. Mean emitted photon energy"),
    p("Average the two equally likely photon energies."),
    sub("4b. Mean emitted photon momentum"),
    p("Find its mean momentum at the retained order relative to the atomic momentum."),
    sub("4c. Mean final atomic energy"),
    p("Find mean atomic energy after emission."),
    sub("4d. Mean final atomic momentum"),
    p("Find the mean final atomic momentum."),
    sub("5a. Mean energy transfer"),
    p("Find the mean atomic energy change in one complete absorption-emission cycle."),
    sub("5b. Mean momentum transfer"),
    p("Find the mean atomic momentum change in one complete cycle."),
    sub("6a. Co-propagating energy transfer"),
    p("Now a resonant laser beam of laboratory frequency \\(\\omega_L^{\\prime}\\) travels along +x with the atom. Find mean energy change per cycle."),
    sub("6b. Co-propagating momentum transfer"),
    p("Find mean momentum change per co-propagating cycle."),
    sub("7a. Two-beam force"),
    fig("batch7-counter-beams", "Original two counterpropagating beams and an atomic gas travelling in the positive x direction."),
    p("For N atoms at rest, a beam produces excited population \\(N_{exc}=N\\Omega_R^2/[(\\omega_0-\\omega_L)^2+\\Gamma^2/4+2\\Omega_R^2]\\), with lifetime \\(\\Gamma^{-1}\\). Thus cycles occur at rate \\(\\Gamma N_{exc}\\). Two opposite beams of the same arbitrary frequency illuminate atoms moving at v. Find their total force, treating the two beam rates independently in this supplied model."),
    sub("8a. Linear friction force"),
    p("Expand the two-beam force to first order in v."),
    sub("8b. Positive force condition"),
    p("For \\(v>0\\), when does the force speed up the atoms?"),
    sub("8c. Zero force condition"),
    p("When is the force zero for the symmetric pair?"),
    sub("8d. Cooling condition"),
    p("For \\(v>0\\), when does the force slow the atoms?"),
    sub("8e. Cooling for opposite motion"),
    p("If the atoms instead move at velocity \\(-v\\), what detuning gives cooling?"),
    sub("9a. Velocity decay"),
    p("For red detuning and initial velocity \\(v_0\\), find velocity after time \\(\\tau\\). Use b for the per-atom coefficient defined in 8a."),
    sub("9b. Temperature decay"),
    p("The atoms initially have thermal equilibrium temperature \\(T_0\\). Find their temperature after time tau in this deterministic low-velocity model."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("DOPPLER LASER COOLING AND OPTICAL MOLASSES"),
    sub("1a. Absorption resonance"),
    p("The approaching atom sees a blueshift: \\(\\omega_0=\\omega_L(1+v/c)\\) to first order."),
    sub("1b. Momentum after absorption"),
    p("Conserve momentum: \\(p_{at}=mv-\\hbar q\\)."),
    sub("1c. Energy after absorption"),
    p("Energy conservation gives \\(E_{at}=mv^2/2+\\hbar\\omega_L\\). Equivalently, \\((mv-\\hbar q)^2/(2m)+\\hbar\\omega_0\\) reduces to this at the retained order."),
    sub("2a. Backward photon energy"),
    p("Backward Doppler redshift cancels the incident resonance blueshift, leaving \\(E_\\gamma\\simeq\\hbar\\omega_L\\)."),
    sub("2b. Backward photon momentum"),
    p("\\(p_\\gamma=-\\hbar\\omega_L/c=-\\hbar q\\)."),
    sub("2c. Atom after backward emission"),
    p("The emitted backward momentum restores the incident loss: \\(p_{at}=mv\\) to retained order."),
    sub("2d. Atom energy after backward emission"),
    p("The atom returns to its internal ground state and original momentum, so \\(E_{at}=mv^2/2\\)."),
    sub("3a. Forward photon energy"),
    p("The emitted light is Doppler blueshifted in addition to the incident resonance shift: \\(E_\\gamma=\\hbar\\omega_L(1+2v/c)\\)."),
    sub("3b. Forward photon momentum"),
    p("\\(p_\\gamma=E_\\gamma/c=\\hbar\\omega_L(1+2v/c)/c\\)."),
    sub("3c. Atom after forward emission"),
    p("\\(p_{at}=mv-2\\hbar q\\). The extra photon Doppler term is a product of the two small ratios when compared with mv, so it is discarded here."),
    sub("3d. Atom energy after forward emission"),
    p("Expand \\((mv-2\\hbar q)^2/(2m)\\) to first order in recoil relative to mv: \\[E_{at}=\\frac12mv^2-2\\hbar qv.\\] This also follows from 1c minus 3a. The official solution's corresponding expression loses a factor two in the kinetic-energy change. The native key uses the conservation-consistent result."),
    sub("4a. Mean emitted photon energy"),
    p("\\(\\overline E_\\gamma=\\hbar\\omega_L(1+v/c)\\)."),
    sub("4b. Mean emitted photon momentum"),
    p("The residual \\(\\hbar qv/c\\) is a product of the two small ratios relative to mv, so the retained mean emitted momentum is zero."),
    sub("4c. Mean final atomic energy"),
    p("Average 2d and the corrected 3d: \\[\\overline E_{at}=mv^2/2-\\hbar qv.\\] The source's half-sized average loss inherits the factor error in 3d."),
    sub("4d. Mean final atomic momentum"),
    p("Average mv and \\(mv-2\\hbar q\\), yielding \\(mv-\\hbar q\\)."),
    sub("5a. Mean energy transfer"),
    p("\\[\\Delta E=-\\hbar qv=-\\hbar\\omega_Lv/c.\\] It equals v times mean momentum transfer to the retained order. The official answer has an erroneous factor one half, inconsistent with both conservation and 5b."),
    sub("5b. Mean momentum transfer"),
    p("\\(\\Delta p=-\\hbar q\\)."),
    sub("6a. Co-propagating energy transfer"),
    p("The resonance condition is \\(\\omega_0=\\omega_L^{\\prime}(1-v/c)\\). Reversing the photon momentum reverses the first-order work, giving \\(\\Delta E=+\\hbar\\omega_L^{\\prime}v/c\\). This corrects the same factor-one-half error in the official answer."),
    sub("6b. Co-propagating momentum transfer"),
    p("\\(\\Delta p=+\\hbar\\omega_L^{\\prime}/c\\)."),
    sub("7a. Two-beam force"),
    p("Let \\(\\delta=\\omega_0-\\omega_L\\), \\(q=\\omega_L/c\\), \\(D=\\Gamma^2/4+2\\Omega_R^2\\). The +x beam is seen at \\(\\omega_L-qv\\), and the -x beam at \\(\\omega_L+qv\\). Thus \\[F=N\\Gamma\\hbar q\\Omega_R^2\\left[\\frac1{(\\delta+qv)^2+D}-\\frac1{(\\delta-qv)^2+D}\\right].\\]"),
    sub("8a. Linear friction force"),
    p("Writing \\(D_0=\\delta^2+\\Gamma^2/4+2\\Omega_R^2\\), the denominator difference is \\(-4\\delta qv/D_0^2\\). Hence \\[F=-Nbv,\\qquad b=\\frac{4\\hbar q^2\\Omega_R^2\\Gamma\\delta}{D_0^2}.\\] Here b is the damping coefficient per atom, not the total coefficient Nb."),
    sub("8b. Positive force condition"),
    p("Blue detuning, \\(\\omega_L>\\omega_0\\), gives \\(\\delta<0\\) and positive force."),
    sub("8c. Zero force condition"),
    p("At exact resonance \\(\\omega_L=\\omega_0\\), the two rates are equal. The force is also zero at v = 0 for any detuning."),
    sub("8d. Cooling condition"),
    p("Red detuning, \\(\\omega_L<\\omega_0\\), gives positive b and a force opposite to v."),
    sub("8e. Cooling for opposite motion"),
    p("The linear force is odd in v. The same red-detuning condition \\(\\omega_L<\\omega_0\\) gives a force opposing either velocity sign."),
    sub("9a. Velocity decay"),
    p("Total mass is Nm, so \\(Nm\\dot v=-Nbv\\). Therefore \\[v(\\tau)=v_0e^{-b\\tau/m}.\\] Dividing a total force coefficient by the single-atom mass without cancelling N would be inconsistent."),
    sub("9b. Temperature decay"),
    p("Every velocity component in the one-dimensional model is reduced by \\(e^{-b\\tau/m}\\). Since temperature is proportional to mean-square velocity, \\[T(\\tau)=T_0e^{-2b\\tau/m}.\\] Recoil diffusion and other heating prevent the model from describing arbitrarily low temperatures."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2011-t1"] = {
  statement: [
    h("A Three-body Problem and LISA"),
    p("Two gravitating masses M and m move in coplanar circular orbits of radii R and r about their common centre O, with \\(MR=mr\\). A third infinitesimal mass \\(\\mu\\) is placed in the same plane and rotates so its position relative to M and m is stationary. It is not collinear with them and does not perturb their motion. G denotes the gravitational constant."),
    fig("batch7-three-body-geometry", "Original coplanar circular orbits of M, m and infinitesimal mass mu about their common centre O."),
    sub("1.1. Binary angular velocity"),
    p("Find the angular velocity of the line joining M and m."),
    sub("1.2.1. Distance to M"),
    p("Find the distance from \\(\\mu\\) to M in terms of R and r."),
    sub("1.2.2. Distance to m"),
    p("Find the distance from \\(\\mu\\) to m."),
    sub("1.2.3. Distance to O"),
    p("Find distance \\(\\rho\\) from \\(\\mu\\) to the common centre."),
    sub("1.3. Restricted radial oscillation"),
    p("Now take M = m. Give the infinitesimal mass a small radial perturbation along its line from O. Under the source's assumed conservation of its angular momentum, find the radial oscillation frequency in terms of \\(\\omega_0\\)."),
    sub("1.4. LISA relative speed"),
    fig("batch7-lisa-triangle", "Original Earth and three spacecraft at the corners of an equilateral triangle, with annual rotation directions."),
    p("The source describes three spacecraft at the corners of an equilateral triangle of arm length \\(L=5.0\\) million kilometres. Their plane rolls once per year as they orbit the Sun, trailing Earth by \\(20^\\circ\\). Find the relative speed of two spacecraft in their common plane, using 365 days per year."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("A Three-body Problem and LISA"),
    sub("1.1. Binary angular velocity"),
    p("Force balance for either mass, together with the centre-of-mass relation, gives \\[\\omega_0=\\sqrt{\\frac{G(M+m)}{(R+r)^3}}.\\]"),
    sub("1.2.1. Distance to M"),
    p("In the co-rotating configuration, the acceleration is \\(-\\omega_0^2\\mathbf r_\\mu\\). Equate this to the vector sum of the two gravitational accelerations. Comparing coefficients of the two nonparallel relative-position vectors using \\(M\\mathbf r_M+m\\mathbf r_m=0\\) gives equal inverse-cube distances to the two masses, each equal to \\((R+r)^{-3}\\). Thus the triangle is equilateral and both distances are R + r."),
    sub("1.2.2. Distance to m"),
    p("The same equilateral configuration gives R + r. There are two mirror positions, the usual triangular Lagrange points."),
    sub("1.2.3. Distance to O"),
    p("Use the cosine rule in a triangle with sides r and R+r and included angle \\(60^\\circ\\): \\[\\rho=\\sqrt{R^2+Rr+r^2}.\\]"),
    sub("1.3. Restricted radial oscillation"),
    p("Here R = r and \\(\\rho_0=\\sqrt3R\\). In the stipulated radial model the attraction is \\(-2GM\\rho/(R^2+\\rho^2)^{3/2}\\). Conserved angular momentum gives centrifugal term \\(\\rho_0^4\\omega_0^2/\\rho^3\\). Linearizing their sum around \\(\\rho_0\\) yields \\[\\ddot\\xi=-\\frac74\\omega_0^2\\xi,\\qquad\\omega_{rad}=\\frac{\\sqrt7}{2}\\omega_0.\\] This is the restricted radial calculation requested. It does not establish stability of the unrestricted equal-mass triangular three-body configuration."),
    sub("1.4. LISA relative speed"),
    p("For rigid rotation in the triangle plane, the relative-position vector has fixed length L and rotates with \\(\\omega=2\\pi/T\\). Hence \\[v_{rel}=\\omega L=\\frac{2\\pi(5.0\\times10^9\\,\\mathrm m)}{365\\times86400\\,\\mathrm s}=996\\,\\mathrm{m/s}.\\] The common heliocentric translation cancels. These are the mission parameters supplied in the historical problem, not a statement of the current mission design."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2013-t3"] = {
  statement: [
    h("The Greenlandic Ice Sheet"),
    p("Model Greenland as a rectangular island of width \\(2L\\), length \\(5L\\), with a flat bed at sea level. Ice height H(x) is independent of y, symmetric about the divide x=0, and vanishes at x=±L. Use \\(\\rho_i=917\\,\\mathrm{kg/m^3}\\), \\(\\rho_w=998\\,\\mathrm{kg/m^3}\\), \\(g=9.82\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}\\), basal friction stress \\(S_b=100\\,\\mathrm{kPa}\\). Neglect atmospheric pressure. The integral \\(\\int_0^1\\sqrt{1-u}\\,du=2/3\\) may be used. In the dynamical model, incompressible ice flows in the xz plane. Central snowfall adds ice thickness c per year, balanced by melting at the coasts. Near the divide take H≈Hm constant, vx independent of z, vz independent of x and vz=0 at the bed. The historical data and idealizations below are those of the 2013 problem."),
    fig("batch7-ice-geometry", "Original Greenland map, rectangular island dimensions 2L and 5L, and vertical ice cross-section H(x) with a thin slab."),
    sub("3.1. Hydrostatic pressure"),
    p("Find p(x,z) at height z above the bed."),
    sub("3.2a. Slab force balance"),
    p("For x>0, balance horizontal pressure force on a slab of base ΔxΔy against \\(S_b\\Delta x\\Delta y\\). Write \\(S_b=kH\\,dH/dx\\) and determine k."),
    sub("3.2b. Ice height profile"),
    p("Find H(x) and the maximum height Hm. Show the scaling with L."),
    sub("3.2c. Volume scaling exponent"),
    p("The island area is A=10L². Find γ in \\(V_{ice}\\propto A^\\gamma\\), and derive the volume coefficient."),
    sub("3.3. Horizontal ice velocity"),
    p("Use mass conservation in the central region to find vx(x)."),
    sub("3.4. Vertical ice velocity"),
    p("Using \\(\\partial_xv_x+\\partial_zv_z=0\\), find vz(z)."),
    sub("3.5. Particle trajectory"),
    p("A particle starts at \\((x_i,H_m)\\). Find its path z(x) in the central approximation."),
    sub("3.6. Age at the divide"),
    p("Find the age τ(z) of ice at height z above ground at x=0."),
    sub("3.7a. Accumulation rates"),
    p("A 3060 m core has the end of the ice age at depth 1492 m, 11,700 years ago. The ice age began 120,000 years ago at depth 3040 m. Assume Hm=3060 m throughout and constant rates c_ig and c_ia in each period. Find both in m/year."),
    sub("3.7b. Temperature transition"),
    fig("batch7-ice-isotopes", "Original oxygen isotope calibration against temperature and isotope data against core depth, with labelled ticks and units."),
    p("The supplied graphs relate \\(\\delta^{18}O=1000(R_{ice}/R_{ref}-1)\\) per mille to temperature, with R=[18O]/[16O] and an equatorial-ocean reference. Use the core and calibration plots to estimate the temperature change at the ice-age/interglacial transition."),
    sub("3.8. Uniform sea level rise"),
    p("Use Greenland area \\(A_G=1.71\\times10^{12}\\,\\mathrm{m^2}\\), ocean area \\(A_O=3.61\\times10^{14}\\,\\mathrm{m^2}\\) and the height profile to find the mean rise after all ice melts."),
    sub("3.9. Local gravitational tide"),
    p("Model Greenland ice as a point mass at Earth's surface. Copenhagen is 3500 km along the surface from it. With a global ocean, no rotation, \\(M_E=5.97\\times10^{24}\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\), \\(R_E=6.38\\times10^6\\,\\mathrm m\\), find the sea-level difference between Copenhagen and the point antipodal to Greenland. Work to first order in sea height."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("The Greenlandic Ice Sheet"),
    sub("3.1. Hydrostatic pressure"),
    p("Integrating \\(\\partial p/\\partial z=-\\rho_i g\\) with zero surface pressure gives \\(p=\\rho_i g[H(x)-z]\\)."),
    sub("3.2a. Slab force balance"),
    p("The integrated pressure force per transverse length is \\(\\rho_i gH^2/2\\). Its decrease across the slab gives \\(-\\rho_i gH H^{\\prime}\\Delta x\\Delta y=S_b\\Delta x\\Delta y\\). Thus \\(k=-\\rho_i g\\), including the sign on the positive-x side."),
    sub("3.2b. Ice height profile"),
    p("Integrate \\(H H^{\\prime}=-S_b/(\\rho_i g)\\) from the coast: \\[H(x)=\\sqrt{\\frac{2S_b}{\\rho_i g}(L-|x|)},\\qquad H_m=\\sqrt{\\frac{2S_bL}{\\rho_i g}}\\propto L^{1/2}.\\]"),
    sub("3.2c. Volume scaling exponent"),
    p("\\(V_{ice}=5L\\int_{-L}^{L}H(x)dx=(20/3)H_mL^2=(2/3)AH_m\\). Since Hm∝L^(1/2), volume scales as L^(5/2) and \\(\\gamma=5/4\\)."),
    sub("3.3. Horizontal ice velocity"),
    p("Snowfall on a strip from 0 to x supplies volume cx per unit transverse length per year. Outward transport is Hm vx. Thus \\(v_x=cx/H_m\\)."),
    sub("3.4. Vertical ice velocity"),
    p("\\(dv_z/dz=-c/H_m\\), and the bed condition fixes \\(v_z=-cz/H_m\\)."),
    sub("3.5. Particle trajectory"),
    p("Dividing the velocities gives \\(dz/dx=-z/x\\), hence \\(xz=H_mx_i\\) and \\(z=H_mx_i/x\\)."),
    sub("3.6. Age at the divide"),
    p("Integrate \\(dz/dt=-cz/H_m\\) from Hm to z: \\[\\tau(z)=\\frac{H_m}{c}\\ln\\frac{H_m}{z}.\\]"),
    sub("3.7a. Accumulation rates"),
    p("For the interglacial period, \\(c_{ig}=3060\\ln(3060/1568)/11700=0.175\\,\\mathrm{m/year}\\). At the period boundary a particle currently at z=20 m was at \\(20(3060/1568)\\) m. Equivalently \\(c_{ia}=3060\\ln(1568/20)/(120000-11700)=0.123\\,\\mathrm{m/year}\\). The two age intervals must be treated separately."),
    sub("3.7b. Temperature transition"),
    p("The isotope signal rises from approximately −43.5 to −34.5 per mille. The calibration maps these to roughly −40°C and −28°C, giving a warming of about 12 K. Graph-reading uncertainty is expected."),
    sub("3.8. Uniform sea level rise"),
    p("\\(L=\\sqrt{A_G/10}=4.14\\times10^5\\,\\mathrm m\\), \\(H_m\\simeq3030\\,\\mathrm m\\), \\(V_{ice}=(2/3)A_GH_m=3.45\\times10^{15}\\,\\mathrm{m^3}\\). Meltwater volume is multiplied by \\(\\rho_i/\\rho_w\\). The rise is \\(h=(\\rho_i/\\rho_w)V_{ice}/A_O=8.79\\,\\mathrm m\\)."),
    sub("3.9. Local gravitational tide"),
    p("Let \\(M_i=\\rho_iV_{ice}\\simeq3.17\\times10^{18}\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\). At angular distance θ, separation from the ice is \\(r=2R_E\\sin(\\theta/2)\\). Constant potential gives \\(gh-GM_i/r=\\text{constant}\\). Thus \\[h_{CPH}-h_{opp}=\\frac{M_iR_E}{2M_E}\\left[\\csc\\left(\\frac{3500\\times10^3}{2R_E}\\right)-1\\right]\\simeq4.56\\,\\mathrm m.\\] The ocean is locally higher near the ice. Its loss on melting partially offsets the uniform rise."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2016-t2"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Nonlinear Dynamics in Electric Circuits"),
    p("A bistable element X has the piecewise-linear I to U characteristic in the figure. The lower branch goes from (0 V,0 A) to (10 V,1 A). The intermediate branch joins (10 V,1 A) to (4 V,4 A). The upper branch starts at (4 V,4 A) and its extension passes through the origin. Holding voltage Uh=4.00 V and threshold Uth=10.0 V. The supplied circuits use ideal components. All numerical evaluations below use R=3.00 Ω, E=15.0 V, L=1.00 μH for part A and C=1.00 μF for parts B and C. Switching between branches is instantaneous."),
    fig("batch7-bistable-curve", "Original piecewise-linear multivalued current-voltage characteristic with holding voltage 4 V and threshold 10 V."),
    sub("a1. Characteristic parameters"),
    p("Find Roff, Ron, Rint and I0 in the intermediate-branch equation \\(I=I_0-U/R_{int}\\)."),
    sub("a2. Number of stationary states"),
    fig("batch7-inductive-circuit", "Original series non-linear element X, resistor R, inductor L and voltage source E."),
    p("Connect E, R, L and X in series. Show graphically how stationary states are found. How many are possible as E varies for R=3 Ω and R=1 Ω?"),
    sub("a3. Series operating point"),
    p("For R=3 Ω and E=15 V calculate stationary I and U."),
    sub("a4. Inductive stability"),
    p("Determine and justify the stability of the part A3 stationary state."),
    sub("b1. Capacitive limit cycle"),
    fig("batch7-capacitive-circuit", "Original capacitor C in parallel with element X, fed by a voltage source E through resistor R."),
    p("Now remove L and connect C in parallel with X, fed by E through R. Draw the closed oscillation cycle on the I to U diagram, with directions and reasons for the jumps."),
    sub("b2. Oscillation period"),
    p("Derive the voltage evolution on each branch, its dwell time and total period."),
    sub("b3. Power estimate"),
    p("Estimate the mean power dissipated in X to order of magnitude and explain the estimate."),
    sub("b4. Transmitter antenna"),
    p("The oscillation feeds an antenna with a free end. Find its optimal length s below 1 km using c=3.00×10^8 m/s."),
    sub("c1. Neuristor pulse sketches"),
    fig("batch7-neuristor-pulse", "Original supply voltage changing from 12 V to 15 V at t0 and back after duration tau."),
    p("Initially the capacitive circuit is stationary at Etilde=12.0 V. At t0 increase the supply to 15.0 V for duration τ<T, then return to 12.0 V. Sketch IX(t) for τ below and above a critical duration, and explain the difference."),
    sub("c2. Critical pulse duration"),
    p("Derive and evaluate the duration needed to first reach Uth."),
    sub("c3. Firing decision"),
    p("For τ=1.00 μs, does the circuit act as a neuristor? Justify."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Nonlinear Dynamics in Electric Circuits"),
    sub("a1. Characteristic parameters"),
    p("The lower and upper slopes give \\(R_{off}=10.0\\,\\Omega\\), \\(R_{on}=1.00\\,\\Omega\\). The middle slope is −0.5 A/V, so \\(R_{int}=2.00\\,\\Omega\\), \\(I_0=6.00\\,\\mathrm A\\)."),
    sub("a2. Number of stationary states"),
    p("At a stationary state \\(E=RI+U\\). Intersections of \\(I=(E-U)/R\\) with the characteristic give the states. For R=3 Ω there is exactly one. For R=1 Ω there may be one, two at a corner, or three. The two-state corner cases must not be omitted."),
    sub("a3. Series operating point"),
    p("The physical intersection lies on the intermediate branch. \\(I=(E-R_{int}I_0)/(R-R_{int})=3.00\\,\\mathrm A\\), and \\(U=R_{int}(I_0-I)=6.00\\,\\mathrm V\\)."),
    sub("a4. Inductive stability"),
    p("Kirchhoff's law gives \\(L\\dot I=E-R_{int}I_0-(R-R_{int})I\\). A perturbation obeys \\(L\\delta\\dot I=-(R-R_{int})\\delta I\\). Because R−Rint=1 Ω>0 the state is stable, with decay time L/(R−Rint)=1 μs."),
    sub("b1. Capacitive limit cycle"),
    p("Voltage is continuous across the capacitor, so jumps are vertical. On the off branch voltage increases from Uh to Uth, jumps to the on branch at Uth, decreases to Uh, then jumps to the off branch. The middle-branch stationary state is unstable in this capacitive circuit. The branch equilibria lie outside the allowed branch intervals, so motion continues to each corner."),
    sub("b2. Oscillation period"),
    p("For branch resistance r, \\(C\\dot U=(E-U)/R-U/r\\). Define \\(U_r=Er/(R+r)\\), \\(t_r=CRr/(R+r)\\). Then \\(U(t)=U_r+[U(0)-U_r]e^{-t/t_r}\\). Thus \\[t_{on}=t_{R_{on}}\\ln\\frac{U_{th}-U_{on}}{U_h-U_{on}}=2.41\\,\\mu s,\\quad t_{off}=t_{R_{off}}\\ln\\frac{U_{off}-U_h}{U_{off}-U_{th}}=3.71\\,\\mu s.\\] Their sum is T=6.12 μs."),
    sub("b3. Power estimate"),
    p("The on branch dominates. Estimating U by (Uh+Uth)/2 gives \\(\\overline P\\sim[(U_h+U_{th})/2]^2t_{on}/(R_{on}T)\\simeq19\\,\\mathrm W\\). The source accepts justified estimates between 5 and 50 W. This is an estimate rather than an exact integral."),
    sub("b4. Transmitter antenna"),
    p("For the fundamental quarter-wave mode, \\(s=cT/4=459\\,\\mathrm m\\). Other odd quarter-wave lengths exceed 1 km."),
    sub("c1. Neuristor pulse sketches"),
    p("Initially \\(\\widetilde U=12R_{off}/(R+R_{off})=9.23\\,\\mathrm V\\). A short pulse raises U on the off branch but does not reach Uth, so current returns smoothly from above to its original value. A longer pulse reaches Uth, jumps to the on branch, decays there and jumps back at Uh. It then approaches the original off-branch state from below. The firing pulse is an all-or-nothing large excursion."),
    sub("c2. Critical pulse duration"),
    p("Using the off-branch exponential, \\[\\tau_{crit}=\\frac{CRR_{off}}{R+R_{off}}\\ln\\frac{U_{off}-\\widetilde U}{U_{off}-U_{th}}=0.936\\,\\mu s.\\]"),
    sub("c3. Firing decision"),
    p("Yes. Since 1.00 μs exceeds 0.936 μs, the threshold is crossed and one large current excursion occurs before the original stationary state is recovered."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2018-t1"] = {
  statement: [
    h("LIGO-GW150914"),
    p("The historical GW150914 signal is modelled as two masses M1 and M2 on slowly shrinking, nonrelativistic circular orbits. Positions are relative to their centre of mass, \\(M_1\\mathbf r_1+M_2\\mathbf r_2=0\\). Define \\(M=M_1+M_2\\), \\(\\mu=M_1M_2/M\\), separation L and orbital angular velocity Ω. Use G=6.67×10^−11 N m²/kg², c=3.00×10^8 m/s, solar mass 2.00×10^30 kg and solar radius 7.00×10^5 km. The given quadrupole radiation formula is \\[\\mathcal P=\\frac{G}{5c^5}\\sum_{i,j=1}^3(\\dddot Q_{ij})^2,\\quad Q_{ij}=\\sum_{A=1}^2M_A(x_{Ai}x_{Aj}-\\delta_{ij}r_A^2/3).\\] The gravitational-wave cycle frequency is twice the orbital cycle frequency. Treat this Newtonian/quadrupole model as the specified approximation, even near merger."),
    sub("a.1. Newtonian acceleration"),
    p("Write \\(\\ddot{\\mathbf r}_1=-\\alpha\\mathbf r_1/r_1^n\\). Find n and α."),
    sub("a.2. Binary kinetic energy"),
    p("In \\(E=A(\\mu,\\Omega,L)-GM\\mu/L\\), find A."),
    sub("a.3. Circular energy coefficient"),
    p("Write the energy as \\(E=\\beta GM\\mu/L\\). Find β."),
    sub("b.1. Time-dependent quadrupole"),
    p("Choose mass 1 at \\(r_1(\\cos\\Omega t,\\sin\\Omega t)\\). Write \\(Q_{ii}=\\mu L^2(a_i+b_i\\cos kt)/2\\), \\(Q_{ij}=\\mu L^2c_{ij}\\sin kt/2\\) for i≠j. Find k and all coefficients."),
    sub("b.2. Radiated power coefficient"),
    p("Find ξ in \\(\\mathcal P=\\xi G\\mu^2L^4\\Omega^6/c^5\\)."),
    sub("b.3. Chirp mass"),
    p("Use \\(\\dot E=-\\mathcal P\\) to obtain \\((\\dot\\Omega)^3=(3\\xi)^3\\Omega^{11}(GM_c)^5/c^{15}\\). Find Mc in terms of M and μ."),
    sub("b.4. Chirp integration"),
    p("Relate Ω to fGW and integrate to show \\(f_{GW}^{-8/3}=8\\pi^{8/3}\\xi(GM_c/c^3)^{2/3+p}(t_0-t)^{2-p}\\). Determine p."),
    sub("b.5. Mass from the measured chirp"),
    fig("batch7-ligo-chirp", "Original measured LIGO H1 strain as a function of time with the labelled A, B, C and D intervals."),
    p("In the strain graph A,B,C,D occur at t=0.000, 0.009, 0.034, 0.040 s. Estimate fGW at midpoint tAB and tCD, then estimate Mc and total M in solar masses assuming equal component masses."),
    sub("b.6. Compactness and orbital speed"),
    p("At tCD estimate minimum separation L, maximum individual radius Rmax, ratio Rsun/Rmax, and individual orbital speed vcol/c."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("LIGO-GW150914"),
    sub("a.1. Newtonian acceleration"),
    p("\\(\\mathbf r_2=-(M_1/M_2)\\mathbf r_1\\), so separation is \\((M_1+M_2)r_1/M_2\\). Newton's law then gives \\(n=3\\), \\(\\alpha=GM_2^3/(M_1+M_2)^2\\)."),
    sub("a.2. Binary kinetic energy"),
    p("The centre-of-mass identity \\(M_1r_1^2+M_2r_2^2=\\mu L^2\\) gives \\(A=\\mu L^2\\Omega^2/2\\)."),
    sub("a.3. Circular energy coefficient"),
    p("Force balance gives \\(\\Omega^2=GM/L^3\\). Substitution gives \\(E=-GM\\mu/(2L)\\), so β=−1/2."),
    sub("b.1. Time-dependent quadrupole"),
    p("The matrix is \\[Q=\\frac{\\mu L^2}{2}\\begin{pmatrix}1/3+\\cos2\\Omega t&\\sin2\\Omega t&0\\\\\\sin2\\Omega t&1/3-\\cos2\\Omega t&0\\\\0&0&-2/3\\end{pmatrix}.\\] Hence k=2Ω, a=(1/3,1/3,−2/3), b=(1,−1,0), c12=c21=1 and all remaining off-diagonal coefficients vanish."),
    sub("b.2. Radiated power coefficient"),
    p("Third derivatives have amplitude \\(4\\mu L^2\\Omega^3\\). Summing both diagonal and both off-diagonal contributions gives twice its square. Thus \\(\\xi=32/5\\)."),
    sub("b.3. Chirp mass"),
    p("Differentiate \\(E=-GM\\mu/(2L)\\) and \\(L^3\\Omega^2=GM\\) to eliminate L and its derivative. This gives \\(\\dot\\Omega=3\\xi G^{5/3}\\mu M^{2/3}\\Omega^{11/3}/c^5\\), hence \\(M_c=(\\mu^3M^2)^{1/5}\\)."),
    sub("b.4. Chirp integration"),
    p("Because fGW=2Ω/(2π), Ω=πfGW. Integrating \\(\\dot\\Omega=\\chi\\Omega^{11/3}\\) gives \\(\\Omega^{-8/3}=(8/3)\\chi(t_0-t)\\). Therefore \\(f_{GW}^{-8/3}=8\\pi^{8/3}\\xi(GM_c/c^3)^{5/3}(t_0-t)\\), so p=1."),
    sub("b.5. Mass from the measured chirp"),
    p("The indicated intervals are half-periods: fAB=1/(2×0.009)=55.6 Hz and fCD=1/(2×0.006)=83.3 Hz, at 0.0045 s and 0.037 s. Eliminating t0 between the two integrated chirp equations gives t0≈0.054 s and Mc≈30 solar masses. Equal masses give \\(M=4^{3/5}M_c\\simeq69\\) solar masses. Values depend on graph reading and the stated approximation."),
    sub("b.6. Compactness and orbital speed"),
    p("\\(\\Omega=\\pi f_{CD}\\simeq262\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}\\), \\(L=(GM/\\Omega^2)^{1/3}\\simeq500\\,\\mathrm{km}\\). Non-overlap implies Rmax≈L/2≈250 km, so Rsun/Rmax≈3000. Each equal mass moves at \\(v=\\Omega L/2\\simeq7\\times10^4\\,\\mathrm{km/s}\\simeq0.2c\\). These large speeds also expose the limits of the nonrelativistic model."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2018-t2"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Where is the neutrino?"),
    p("The historical ATLAS model uses a uniform B=2.00 T solenoidal field in a cylinder of radius R=1.10 m. Electrons start at its centre. Use m=9.11×10^−31 kg, e=1.60×10^−19 C, c=3.00×10^8 m/s and ε0=8.85×10^−12 F/m. For perpendicular acceleration the radiated power is \\(P=e^2a^2\\gamma^4/(6\\pi\\epsilon_0c^3)\\). Part B reconstructs a neutrino from \\(t\\to bW^+\\to b\\mu^+\\nu\\). Treat all final particles and jets as massless. Their measured momenta (px,py,pz), in GeV/c, are: anti-muon (−24.7,−24.9,−12.4), jet 1 (−14.2,50.1,94.1), neutrino (−104.1,5.3,unknown). The neutrino transverse components come from missing transverse momentum. Do not assume zero total longitudinal momentum. Use mW=80.4 GeV/c² and rest-frame top lifetime 5×10^−25 s."),
    sub("a.1. Classical cyclotron radius"),
    p("For a nonrelativistic electron with kinetic energy K, derive its radius r."),
    sub("a.2. Escape momentum"),
    p("What minimum momentum lets an electron produced at the centre reach the cylindrical boundary? Give MeV/c."),
    sub("a.3. Ultrarelativistic radiation"),
    p("Write \\(P=\\xi e^4E^2B^2/(\\epsilon_0m^kc^n)\\). Find ξ,k,n."),
    sub("a.4. Energy loss coefficient"),
    p("In \\(E(t)=E_0/(1+\\alpha E_0t)\\), find α."),
    sub("a.5. Radiated energy on escape"),
    p("An electron starts radially with 100 GeV energy. Estimate the positive energy lost before escape, in MeV."),
    sub("a.6. Time-dependent angular frequency"),
    p("Find the ultrarelativistic cyclotron angular frequency ω(t), using α from A4."),
    sub("b.1. W mass constraint"),
    fig("batch7-atlas-event", "Original ATLAS coordinate axes and reconstructed collision decay products including the escaping neutrino."),
    p("Derive the equation for mW² in terms of the anti-muon and neutrino transverse vectors and their longitudinal components."),
    sub("b.2. Two neutrino solutions"),
    p("Solve the W mass constraint for both possible neutrino pz values."),
    sub("b.3. Reconstructed top masses"),
    p("Calculate the top mass for each neutrino solution. If necessary use approximate fallback pz=70 and −180 GeV/c and state that choice."),
    sub("b.4. Select the likely candidate"),
    fig("batch7-top-distribution", "Original measured top-quark mass distribution with data points, dashed signal and shaded background."),
    p("Use the supplied top-mass distribution to choose a candidate and estimate its normalized signal weight, called probability in the source."),
    sub("b.5. Distance before decay"),
    p("For the likely candidate, calculate the mean laboratory distance travelled before decay."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Where is the neutrino?"),
    sub("a.1. Classical cyclotron radius"),
    p("From \\(evB=mv^2/r\\) and \\(K=mv^2/2\\), \\(r=\\sqrt{2mK}/(eB)\\)."),
    sub("a.2. Escape momentum"),
    p("The track's largest radial distance is twice its curvature radius, so r=R/2=0.55 m. The relativistic relation p=eBr gives p≈330 MeV/c."),
    sub("a.3. Ultrarelativistic radiation"),
    p("For v≈c, \\(a=ecB/(\\gamma m)\\) and E=γmc². Substitution yields \\(P=e^4E^2B^2/(6\\pi\\epsilon_0m^4c^5)\\). Thus ξ=1/(6π), k=4, n=5."),
    sub("a.4. Energy loss coefficient"),
    p("Integrating \\(\\dot E=-\\alpha E^2\\) gives the stated law with \\(\\alpha=e^4B^2/(6\\pi\\epsilon_0m^4c^5)\\)."),
    sub("a.5. Radiated energy on escape"),
    p("Its curvature radius is about 167 m, so flight time is R/c. Loss \\(E_0-E(R/c)\\simeq\\alpha E_0^2R/c\\simeq56\\,\\mathrm{MeV}\\). The signed energy change is −56 MeV, while the requested lost energy is positive."),
    sub("a.6. Time-dependent angular frequency"),
    p("\\(r=p/(eB)\\), p≈E/c, hence \\(\\omega=c/r=ec^2B/E(t)=ec^2B(1+\\alpha E_0t)/E_0\\)."),
    sub("b.1. W mass constraint"),
    p("Add their four-momenta and use zero individual masses: \\[m_W^2c^2=2\\left[p_\\mu\\sqrt{p_{\\nu T}^2+p_{\\nu z}^2}-\\mathbf p_{\\mu T}\\cdot\\mathbf p_{\\nu T}-p_{\\mu z}p_{\\nu z}\\right],\\quad p_\\mu=\\sqrt{p_{\\mu T}^2+p_{\\mu z}^2}.\\]"),
    sub("b.2. Two neutrino solutions"),
    p("Let \\(A=m_W^2c^2/2+\\mathbf p_{\\mu T}\\cdot\\mathbf p_{\\nu T}\\). Squaring \\(p_\\mu\\sqrt{p_{\\nu T}^2+z^2}=A+p_{\\mu z}z\\) gives \\[p_{\\mu T}^2z^2-2Ap_{\\mu z}z+p_\\mu^2p_{\\nu T}^2-A^2=0.\\] Check both roots in the unsquared equation. They are approximately +74.0 and −188.3 GeV/c."),
    sub("b.3. Reconstructed top masses"),
    p("For each root add the three final energies \\(E_t=c(p_\\mu+p_\\nu+p_j)\\) and vectors \\(\\mathbf p_t=\\mathbf p_\\mu+\\mathbf p_\\nu+\\mathbf p_j\\). Then \\(m_t=c^{-2}\\sqrt{E_t^2-c^2p_t^2}\\). The exact-root candidates are about 169.3 and 311.2 GeV/c² respectively. Rounded fallback roots give nearby but different values."),
    sub("b.4. Select the likely candidate"),
    p("The dashed signal curve is about 0.1 near 169.3 GeV/c² and below 0.01 near 311.2 GeV/c², favouring the positive-pz candidate. The plotted normalized bin weight is roughly 0.1. It is not a posterior probability between the two candidates without an additional likelihood model."),
    sub("b.5. Distance before decay"),
    p("Its energy is about 272.6 GeV. Time dilation gives \\(d=v\\gamma\\tau_0=(p_t/m_t)\\tau_0=c\\tau_0\\sqrt{[E_t/(m_tc^2)]^2-1}\\simeq2\\times10^{-16}\\,\\mathrm m\\)."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2018-t3"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Physics of Live Systems"),
    p("Use the following specified physical models. For laminar incompressible flow through a vessel of radius r and length ℓ, \\(\\Delta P=RQ\\), \\(R=8\\eta\\ell/(\\pi r^4)\\). A symmetric tree has N levels numbered 0 through N−1. Each vessel splits into two, with \\(r_{i+1}=r_i/2^{1/3}\\), \\(\\ell_{i+1}=\\ell_i/2^{1/3}\\). The pressure drop across the whole tree is ΔP. Use 760 mmHg=1.013×10^5 Pa. The elastic-vessel equivalent circuit is a series R,L feeding a shunt C, with outlet pressure across C, \\(C=3\\ell\\pi r^3/(2Eh)\\), \\(L=9\\ell\\rho/(4\\pi r^2)\\). Here E is wall Young modulus and h its thickness. The numerical tree has N=6, r0=6.0×10^−5 m, ℓ0=2.0×10^−3 m, inlet pressure 55 mmHg, capillary pressure 30 mmHg, η=3.5×10^−3 kg/(m s), ρ=1.05×10^3 kg/m³, E=0.06 MPa."),
    fig("batch7-arteriole-tree", "Original symmetric branched arteriole tree, levels zero through N minus one and capillary bed pressure."),
    sub("a.1. Flow in each generation"),
    p("Find Qi in terms of ΔP,N,η,r0,ℓ0 and i."),
    sub("a.2. Numerical inlet flow"),
    p("Calculate Q0 in mL/hour."),
    sub("a.3. Pressure amplitude transfer"),
    fig("batch7-vessel-lcr", "Original equivalent vessel circuit with series R and L and shunt capacitance C across the output."),
    p("For sinusoidal inlet pressure of angular frequency ω find Pout/Pin and the condition that low-frequency outlet oscillations are attenuated. Express the condition in η,ρ,E,h,r,ℓ."),
    sub("a.4. Wall thickness bound"),
    p("Assuming the same h at every level, estimate its maximum value for attenuation throughout the numerical tree."),
    sub("b.1. Tumour volume fraction"),
    fig("batch7-tumour-sphere", "Original spherical tumour T of radius RT inside normal tissue N bounded at radius R."),
    p("An inextensible sphere initially contains normal tissue of density ρ0. A central tumour grows, with fixed normal-tissue mass. The densities are ρN=ρ0(1+p/KN), ρT=ρ0(1+p/KT), with common excess pressure p. Define v=VT/V, μ=MT/MN, κ=KN/KT. Find v(μ,κ), selecting the physical branch."),
    sub("b.2. Central temperature"),
    p("A spherical tumour of radius RT generates uniform heating power density P. Tumour and all surrounding tissue have conductivity k. Temperature far away is Tb=310.15 K. Find the steady centre temperature."),
    sub("b.3. Heating all tumour cells"),
    p("For RT=5.0 cm and k=0.60 W/(m K), find the threshold power density to raise every tumour cell from 37°C to above 43°C."),
    sub("b.4. Flow reduction under compression"),
    p("Only level N−1 vessels narrow by positive δr. Their collapse law is \\(p/P_{cap}-1=(p_c/P_{cap}-1)(2-\\delta r/\\delta r_c)\\delta r/\\delta r_c\\). At fixed whole-tree pressure drop and small p−Pcap, find the signed relative flow change δQ/Q in terms of v,KN,N,pc,δrc,rN−1,Pcap."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Physics of Live Systems"),
    sub("a.1. Flow in each generation"),
    p("At level i, \\(Q_i=Q_0/2^i\\), while \\(R_i=2^iR_0\\). Each level therefore drops the same pressure Q0R0. Thus \\[Q_i=\\frac{\\Delta P\\pi r_0^4}{2^{i+3}N\\ell_0\\eta}.\\]"),
    sub("a.2. Numerical inlet flow"),
    p("Convert ΔP=25 mmHg to 3332 Pa. Then Q0≈4.04×10^−10 m³/s≈1.45 mL/hour, conventionally rounded to 1.5."),
    sub("a.3. Pressure amplitude transfer"),
    p("Complex impedances give \\[\\frac{P_{out}}{P_{in}}=\\frac1{\\sqrt{(1-\\omega^2LC)^2+\\omega^2C^2R^2}}.\\] To quadratic order in ω, attenuation requires C²R²−2CL>0, or \\(64\\eta^2\\ell^2/(3Ehr^3\\rho)>1\\). At equality attenuation first appears at fourth order."),
    sub("a.4. Wall thickness bound"),
    p("The bound is \\(h<64\\eta^2\\ell_i^2/(3Er_i^3\\rho)=7.7\\times10^{-5}2^{i/3}\\,\\mathrm m\\). The strictest condition is at i=0, giving h less than about 77 μm."),
    sub("b.1. Tumour volume fraction"),
    p("Normal mass conservation gives p=KN v/(1−v). Eliminating p from the tumour mass relation yields \\((1-\\kappa)v^2-(1+\\mu)v+\\mu=0\\). The branch tending to zero as μ→0 is \\[v=\\frac{1+\\mu-\\sqrt{(1+\\mu)^2-4\\mu(1-\\kappa)}}{2(1-\\kappa)}.\\] At κ=1 the continuous limit is v=μ/(1+μ)."),
    sub("b.2. Central temperature"),
    p("Conservation of heat gives \\(T(r)-T_b=P R_T^3/(3kr)\\) outside and \\(T(r)-T_b=P R_T^2/(2k)-Pr^2/(6k)\\) inside. Match temperature and heat flux at RT. At the centre \\(T(0)=T_b+P R_T^2/(2k)\\)."),
    sub("b.3. Heating all tumour cells"),
    p("The coldest tumour point is its surface, not its centre. \\(T(R_T)-T_b=PR_T^2/(3k)\\). The threshold is \\(P_{min}=3k(6\\,\\mathrm K)/R_T^2=4.32\\,\\mathrm{kW/m^3}\\). Strictly exceeding 43°C requires power above this threshold."),
    sub("b.4. Flow reduction under compression"),
    p("Linearizing the collapse law gives \\(\\delta r=\\delta r_c(p-P_{cap})/[2(p_c-P_{cap})]\\). The last level's resistance increases by 4δr/r, while all N unperturbed levels contribute equally. Hence \\[\\frac{\\delta Q}{Q}=-\\frac{2}{N}\\frac{K_Nv-(1-v)P_{cap}}{(1-v)(p_c-P_{cap})}\\frac{\\delta r_c}{r_{N-1}}.\\] This applies on the narrowing side p>Pcap and is negative. Symmetry gives the same relative change in every level."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2019-t2"] = {
  statement: [
    h("The Physics of a Microwave Oven"),
    p("A model magnetron has a central cathode of radius a and a positive cylindrical anode of radius b, with eight LC resonator cavities. Adjacent cavities carry opposite oscillating charge. Their frequency is f=2.45 GHz. Static magnetic field B0 points out of the cross-section. The alternating electric-field amplitude is roughly one third of the static field, and electrons transfer about 80% of their acquired static-field energy to the oscillations. The resonator figure supplies its dimensions. Treat its slot as a parallel-plate capacitor and its cylindrical cavity as an inductor whose magnetic field is 0.6 times that of an infinite solenoid. Use ε0=8.85×10^−12 F/m, μ0=4π×10^−7 H/m, electron m=9.1×10^−31 kg, charge −e with e=1.6×10^−19 C, and c=3.0×10^8 m/s. For the operating magnetron use b−a=15 mm, B0=0.30 T and maximum kinetic energy 800 eV."),
    fig("batch7-magnetron-cavity", "Original eight-cavity magnetron and three-dimensional resonator with d=1 mm, l=6 mm, R=7 mm and h=17.5 mm."),
    sub("a.1. Cavity resonance estimate"),
    p("Estimate the cavity frequency using the dimensions in Figure 1(b). Use the actual 2.45 GHz for later parts."),
    sub("a.2. Crossed-field trajectories"),
    p("In free space let E=−E0 yhat and B=B0 zhat. Find the drift velocity and sketch two cyclotron periods for initial u=(3E0/B0)xhat and u=−(3E0/B0)xhat."),
    sub("a.3. Gyromotion radius bound"),
    p("Estimate the maximum radius in the approximately inertial drift frame."),
    sub("a.4. Radial drift directions"),
    fig("batch7-magnetron-radial", "Original alternating electric field near the magnetron gap with electron locations A through E."),
    p("Using Figure 2, classify points A,B,C,D,E as drifting towards the anode, towards the cathode, or perpendicular to the radius."),
    sub("a.5. Angular focusing"),
    fig("batch7-magnetron-focusing", "Original alternating electric field with six equal-radius electron positions A through F."),
    p("Using Figure 3, state whether the angular separation of each pair AB,AC,BC,DE,DF,EF increases or decreases."),
    sub("a.6. Electron spokes"),
    fig("batch7-magnetron-spoke", "Original cathode and anode cross-section with labelled radii a and b and one red spoke S."),
    p("Add the other spokes to Figure 4, indicate their rotation and find their average angular speed."),
    sub("a.7. Operating voltage"),
    p("Halfway across the gap approximate the total radial field by V0/(b−a), with radial spokes. Find the static voltage needed to match the spoke speed."),
    sub("b.1. Dipole torque and work"),
    p("A fixed-magnitude dipole p0 makes angle θ(t) with E(t)xhat. Give the torque magnitude and the instantaneous power delivered by the field."),
    sub("b.2. Mean dielectric heating"),
    p("For E=E0 sin(ωt)xhat and polarization P=βε0E0 sin(ωt−δ)xhat, find mean absorbed power per volume."),
    sub("b.3. Attenuation with depth"),
    p("The relative dielectric constant is εr, refractive index n=√εr and wave speed c/n. Electric energy density is εrε0E²/2, and mean magnetic and electric energies are equal. Find mean intensity I(z) in terms of I(0)."),
    sub("b.4. Polarization coefficient"),
    p("For small δ the field varies as \\(E_0e^{-nk_0z\\tan\\delta/2}\\sin(nk_0z-\\omega t)\\), with k0=ω/c and tanδ=εloss/εr. Use tanδ≈sinδ to find β."),
    sub("b.5. Penetration depth and temperature"),
    fig("batch7-dielectric-data", "Original dielectric constant and loss coefficient against frequency and wavelength for pure water and dilute salt solution at labelled temperatures."),
    p("Use Figure 5 at 2.45 GHz to find the half-power depth in pure water at 20°C, and state how penetration changes with temperature in pure water and dilute salt solution."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("The Physics of a Microwave Oven"),
    sub("a.1. Cavity resonance estimate"),
    p("If cavity radius is R, height h, slot length ℓ and width d, \\(L=0.6\\pi\\mu_0R^2/h\\), \\(C=\\epsilon_0\\ell h/d\\). Therefore \\[f_{est}=\\frac{c}{2\\pi R}\\sqrt{\\frac d{0.6\\pi\\ell}}\\simeq2.0\\,\\mathrm{GHz}.\\] Height cancels. This approximate geometry need not reproduce 2.45 GHz exactly."),
    sub("a.2. Crossed-field trajectories"),
    p("\\(\\mathbf u_D=\\mathbf E\\times\\mathbf B/B^2=-(E_0/B_0)\\hat x\\). Set ωc=eB0/m. If initial relative x velocity is vprime, then \\[x(t)=-E_0t/B_0+(v^{\\prime}/\\omega_c)\\sin\\omega_ct,\\quad y(t)=(v^{\\prime}/\\omega_c)(1-\\cos\\omega_ct).\\] The two values are vprime=4E0/B0 and −2E0/B0, so radii are 4mE0/(eB0²) and 2mE0/(eB0²). Plot for 0<t<4π/ωc. Both have the same negative-x drift, with differently oriented loops."),
    sub("a.3. Gyromotion radius bound"),
    p("The relative circular speed is at most the laboratory maximum speed. Hence \\(r\\lesssim\\sqrt{2mK_{max}}/(eB_0)=0.318\\,\\mathrm{mm}\\). It is much smaller than the 15 mm gap, supporting the pure-drift approximation."),
    sub("a.4. Radial drift directions"),
    p("Use \\(\\mathbf u_D=\\mathbf E\\times\\mathbf B/B^2\\). Only the azimuthal electric field gives radial drift. A and B drift towards the cathode. C,D,E drift towards the anode. None of these five points is in the perpendicular-only category. The radial static field contributes only azimuthal drift."),
    sub("a.5. Angular focusing"),
    p("At equal radius the common static drift cancels from relative angular velocities. The radial alternating field focuses A,B,C and defocuses D,E,F. Thus AB,AC,BC decrease in angular separation, while DE,DF,EF increase."),
    sub("a.6. Electron spokes"),
    p("There are four spokes separated by π/2. They rotate counterclockwise. In one half-period the field flips and a spoke advances to the adjacent cavity, by π/4. Thus \\(\\omega_s=(\\pi/4)/(1/2f)=\\pi f/2=3.85\\times10^9\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}\\)."),
    sub("a.7. Operating voltage"),
    p("At r=(a+b)/2 the drift speed V0/[B0(b−a)] equals rωs. Hence \\[V_0=\\frac{\\pi fB_0}{4}(b^2-a^2).\\] This is a model estimate of the minimum operating voltage."),
    sub("b.1. Dipole torque and work"),
    p("The restoring signed torque is \\(\\tau_z=-p_0E(t)\\sin\\theta\\), so its magnitude is \\(|p_0E\\sin\\theta|\\). Power is \\(H_i=\\tau_z\\dot\\theta=-p_0E\\sin\\theta\\dot\\theta=E\\,d(p_0\\cos\\theta)/dt\\). Torque magnitude alone must not be used for signed work."),
    sub("b.2. Mean dielectric heating"),
    p("Power density is \\(H=E\\dot P\\). Averaging the product of sine and phase-shifted cosine gives \\[\\langle H\\rangle=\\tfrac12\\beta\\epsilon_0\\omega E_0^2\\sin\\delta.\\]"),
    sub("b.3. Attenuation with depth"),
    p("Total mean energy density is εrε0E0(z)²/2, so \\(I=\\sqrt{\\epsilon_r}\\epsilon_0cE_0(z)^2/2\\). Energy balance gives \\(dI/dz=-\\langle H\\rangle=-\\beta\\omega\\sin\\delta\\,I/(c\\sqrt{\\epsilon_r})\\). Thus \\[I(z)=I(0)\\exp[-\\beta\\omega z\\sin\\delta/(c\\sqrt{\\epsilon_r})].\\]"),
    sub("b.4. Polarization coefficient"),
    p("Squaring the field gives intensity attenuation exponent \\(-\\sqrt{\\epsilon_r}\\omega z\\tan\\delta/c\\). Match this with B3 to obtain β=εr in the stated approximation."),
    sub("b.5. Penetration depth and temperature"),
    p("At 20°C read εr≈78 and εloss≈10. Then \\[z_{1/2}=\\frac{c\\sqrt{\\epsilon_r}\\ln2}{\\omega\\epsilon_{loss}}\\simeq12\\,\\mathrm{mm}.\\] In pure water εloss decreases strongly on warming, so penetration increases. In dilute salt solution εloss increases while εr decreases, so penetration decreases. These are trends at the fixed specified frequency, not all frequencies."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2021-t1"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Planetary Physics"),
    p("Neglect planetary surface curvature and use \\((1+x)^a\\simeq1+ax\\) for |x|≪1. Part A begins with two vertical plates of width w whose lower edges are h below the surrounding water surface. Oil of density ρoil<ρ0 is poured between the plates until its lower surface reaches their lower edges. Plates and vessel walls are tall enough to prevent overflow. Neglect mixing and surface tension. Next model the mid-ocean ridge in the figure as invariant along a length L in y. The crust is thin compared with its horizontal variation and behaves as a freely bendable plate. The mantle flows as an incompressible fluid. At x=0 crust thickness is zero. At infinity it is D and the sea bed has subsided by h. The origin is the ridge summit and z points downwards. Water density and temperature are ρ0,T0 and mantle values are ρ1,T1. Crustal length varies as \\(\\ell(T)=\\ell_1[1-k_l(T_1-T)/(T_1-T_0)]\\), with |kl|≪1. Thermal conductivity κ and specific heat c are constant."),
    fig("batch7-oil-plates", "Original parallel vertical plates with water alone and with an oil column, showing immersion depth h and width w."),
    sub("a.1. Oil force on a plate"),
    p("Find the signed horizontal net force on the right plate, taking rightwards as positive."),
    sub("a.2. Thermal density law"),
    fig("batch7-ridge-profile", "Original mid-ocean ridge section showing water, crust, mantle, coordinate axes, subsidence h and far-field crust thickness D."),
    p("Find the exact isotropic density law and its first-order form \\(\\rho(T)\\simeq\\rho_1[1+k(T_1-T)/(T_1-T_0)]\\). Identify k."),
    sub("a.3. Asymptotic crust thickness"),
    p("Far from the ridge the temperature varies linearly across the crust from T0 to T1. Use hydrostatic mantle and water pressures to find D."),
    sub("a.4. Horizontal force on the crust"),
    p("Find the leading-order horizontal force on the right half of the crust in terms of ρ0,ρ1,h,L,k,g. Treat the mantle pressure as hydrostatic and use the source's leading-order thin-crust model."),
    sub("a.5. Thermal relaxation time"),
    p("If the crust is thermally isolated from the rest of Earth, estimate the timescale for its upper and lower temperatures to approach one another. Assume independence from the two initial temperatures."),
    sub("b.1. Returning seismic ray"),
    fig("batch7-seismic-axes", "Original line earthquake source at the surface with horizontal x and downward z axes."),
    p("A line source at x=z=0 emits a ray at angle 0<θ0<π/2 from the downward vertical into a medium of speed v(z)=v0(1+z/z0). Its path is a circle arc. Find its next surface coordinate x1=A cot(bθ0), determining A,b."),
    sub("b.2. Absorbed seismic energy density"),
    p("The source releases energy E per unit length uniformly into the downward half-plane. Waves are absorbed on their first return to the surface. Find and sketch ε(x), energy per surface area."),
    sub("b.3. Furthest shadow zone"),
    p("Now reflect waves perfectly at the surface. Emit only the small angular interval θ0±δθ0/2, with δθ0≪θ0 and ≪1. To leading order find the furthest surface distance that is not reached by this signal."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Planetary Physics"),
    sub("a.1. Oil force on a plate"),
    p("Bottom pressure matching gives oil column height \\(h_o=\\rho_0h/\\rho_{oil}\\). Integrating triangular pressure distributions on both sides yields \\[F_x=\\frac{\\rho_0gh^2w}{2}\\left(\\frac{\\rho_0}{\\rho_{oil}}-1\\right)>0.\\]"),
    sub("a.2. Thermal density law"),
    p("Each dimension changes by the same factor, so \\[\\rho(T)=\\rho_1[1-k_l(T_1-T)/(T_1-T_0)]^{-3}\\simeq\\rho_1[1+3k_l(T_1-T)/(T_1-T_0)].\\] Thus k=3kl."),
    sub("a.3. Asymptotic crust thickness"),
    p("The same mantle depth has equal pressure on the ridge axis and at infinity: \\(\\rho_1(h+D)=\\rho_0h+\\rho_1D(1+k/2)\\). Therefore \\[D=\\frac{2h}{k}(1-\\rho_0/\\rho_1).\\]"),
    sub("a.4. Horizontal force on the crust"),
    p("Integrating the pressure tractions on the sloping upper and lower surfaces gives the difference of their projected vertical integrals. With common pressure pO at z=0, \\[F=L\\int_0^{h+D}(p_O+\\rho_1gz)dz-L\\int_0^h(p_O+\\rho_0gz)dz.\\] Thus \\(F=Lp_OD+L\\rho_1g(h+D)^2/2-L\\rho_0gh^2/2\\). Since D∝1/k and pO and h are fixed, the leading term is \\[F\\simeq\\frac{2gLh^2(\\rho_1-\\rho_0)^2}{k^2\\rho_1},\\] directed away from the ridge. This is the force from the exterior fluids on the specified crust region."),
    sub("a.5. Thermal relaxation time"),
    p("The heat per area to redistribute is of order ρ1cDΔT, and conductive flux is of order κΔT/D. Their ratio gives \\(\\tau\\sim\\rho_1cD^2/\\kappa\\). Dimensional analysis determines the same scaling, with an undetermined order-one prefactor. The temperature difference cancels."),
    sub("b.1. Returning seismic ray"),
    p("Snell's law gives \\(\\sin\\theta/(1+z/z_0)=\\sin\\theta_0\\). The circle radius is \\(R=z_0/\\sin\\theta_0\\), with centre at depth −z0. Its surface chord is \\(x_1=2R\\cos\\theta_0=2z_0\\cot\\theta_0\\). Thus A=2z0,b=1."),
    sub("b.2. Absorbed seismic energy density"),
    p("An angular interval dθ carries E dθ/π. Since \\(|dx/d\\theta|=2z_0\\csc^2\\theta\\), \\[\\epsilon(x)=\\frac{2Ez_0}{\\pi(4z_0^2+x^2)}.\\] The plot is even, peaks at E/(2πz0) at the origin, decays as 1/x², and integrates to E over the entire surface."),
    sub("b.3. Furthest shadow zone"),
    p("The nth return illuminates an interval centred at n x1 with width n|x1′|δθ0. Adjacent intervals first overlap when \\(n\\sim x_1/(|x_1^{\\prime}|\\delta\\theta_0)\\). Thus the last gap lies near \\[x_{max}\\simeq\\frac{x_1^2}{|x_1^{\\prime}|\\delta\\theta_0}=\\frac{2z_0\\cos^2\\theta_0}{\\delta\\theta_0}.\\] This is a leading narrow-beam estimate, with discrete-reflection corrections."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2021-t3"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Particles and Waves"),
    p("Use h=6.626×10^−34 J s, ħ=h/(2π), c=2.998×10^8 m/s, electron mass me=9.109×10^−31 kg, e=1.602×10^−19 C, ε0=8.854×10^−12 F/m, kB=1.381×10^−23 J/K and atomic mass unit u=1.661×10^−27 kg. A quantum particle in a one-dimensional infinite well has V=0 for 0≤x≤L and infinite V outside. Its de Broglie standing wave has nodes at both walls. Photon transitions conserve energy. Later parts use the specified simple molecular, gas and optical-lattice models. The standing-wave condition is \\(2L=n\\lambda_{dB}\\), with \\(p=h/\\lambda_{dB}\\)."),
    sub("a.1. Ground-state energy"),
    p("Find the smallest positive energy in the box."),
    sub("a.2. All box levels"),
    p("Find En for n=1,2,3,... ."),
    sub("a.3. First emission wavelength"),
    p("Find the wavelength emitted in the transition E2→E1."),
    sub("b.1. Cy5 absorption edge"),
    fig("batch7-cy5-backbone", "Original Cy5 chemical structure, conjugated backbone with mean spacing l and effective 10.5 l potential well."),
    p("The Cy5 backbone contributes ten noninteracting electrons to a well of length L=10.5ℓ, where ℓ=140 pm. Include Pauli exclusion and spin. Find the longest absorbed wavelength from the electronic ground state, in terms of ℓ and constants, and numerically."),
    sub("b.2. Cy3 spectral shift"),
    p("Cy3 has two fewer carbon atoms, eight mobile electrons and a well shorter by 2ℓ. Determine the direction and magnitude of the shift relative to Cy5."),
    sub("b.3. Spontaneous emission rate"),
    p("Use dimensions to express the positive spontaneous decay rate K in ε0,h, wavelength λ and transition dipole moment d. The dimensionless prefactor is 16π³/3."),
    sub("b.4. Cy5 lifetime"),
    p("For d≈2.4eℓ and the Cy5 wavelength, estimate τ=1/K."),
    sub("c.1. Thermal momentum and wavelength"),
    p("For a noninteracting gas of 87Rb atoms in thermal equilibrium, estimate typical momentum and de Broglie wavelength from rms speed."),
    sub("c.2. Condensation estimate"),
    p("Estimate spacing ℓ in a gas of number density n. Setting it equal to the rms de Broglie wavelength, find critical temperature Tc."),
    sub("c.3. Critical density and comparison"),
    p("At Tc=100 nK compute nc for mass m=87u. Compare with n0 at 300 K and 10^5 Pa by giving n0 and n0/nc."),
    sub("d.1. Three-beam lattice potential"),
    fig("batch7-three-beam", "Original three interfering laser directions, two sets of hexagonal symmetry axes and an example saddle surface."),
    p("Three equal-amplitude plane waves of wavenumber k, frequency ω and z polarization travel in the xy plane at 120° intervals. Choose k1=k(1,0), k2=k(−1/2,√3/2), k3=k(−1/2,−√3/2). Each field is E0 cos(ki·r−ωt). For V=−α〈E²〉, α>0, find V as a constant plus three cosines and identify their vectors."),
    sub("d.2. Sixfold symmetry"),
    p("Prove invariance under rotations by 60° around the origin."),
    sub("d.3. Axis profiles and extrema"),
    p("Find VX(x)=V(x,0), VY(y)=V(0,y) and one representative of each periodically repeated minimum and maximum of these one-variable functions."),
    sub("d.4. True lattice minima"),
    p("Eliminate saddle points and identify the six true minima nearest to the origin. Express lattice spacing a in laser wavelength λlas."),
    sub("d.5. Rydberg quantum number"),
    p("Estimate the principal quantum number n for a Rb Rydberg electron with classical circular radius equal to λlas=380 nm. Use angular momentum nħ and one effective positive elementary core charge. Give formula and number."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Particles and Waves"),
    sub("a.1. Ground-state energy"),
    p("The longest standing-wave wavelength is 2L, giving p=h/(2L) and \\(E_{min}=h^2/(8mL^2)\\)."),
    sub("a.2. All box levels"),
    p("Fit n half-wavelengths into L: λn=2L/n. Thus \\(E_n=h^2n^2/(8mL^2)\\)."),
    sub("a.3. First emission wavelength"),
    p("The photon energy is \\(3h^2/(8mL^2)\\). Therefore \\(\\lambda_{21}=hc/(E_2-E_1)=8mcL^2/(3h)\\)."),
    sub("b.1. Cy5 absorption edge"),
    p("Two opposite-spin electrons fit each spatial level, filling n=1 to 5. The smallest allowed excitation is 5→6, giving \\[\\lambda=\\frac{8m_ec(10.5\\ell)^2}{11h}=\\frac{882m_ec\\ell^2}{11h}\\simeq647\\,\\mathrm{nm}.\\]"),
    sub("b.2. Cy3 spectral shift"),
    p("Cy3 fills four levels and absorbs in the 4→5 transition. With L=8.5ℓ, \\(\\lambda_{Cy3}=8m_ec(8.5\\ell)^2/(9h)\\simeq518\\,\\mathrm{nm}\\). The absorption edge shifts about 129 nm towards blue. The electron count must change along with box length."),
    sub("b.3. Spontaneous emission rate"),
    p("The dimensions force \\(K\\propto\\epsilon_0^{-1}h^{-1}\\lambda^{-3}d^2\\). Thus \\[K=\\frac{16\\pi^3d^2}{3\\epsilon_0h\\lambda^3}.\\] As a positive decay rate, K satisfies dN/dt=−KN. The source's unsigned definition omits that minus sign."),
    sub("b.4. Cy5 lifetime"),
    p("Use the Cy5 emission/absorption-edge wavelength in the rate expression. \\(\\tau=3\\epsilon_0h\\lambda^3/[16\\pi^3(2.4e\\ell)^2]\\simeq3.3\\,\\mathrm{ns}\\)."),
    sub("c.1. Thermal momentum and wavelength"),
    p("Equipartition gives p²/(2m)=3kBT/2. Hence \\(p=\\sqrt{3mk_BT}\\), \\(\\lambda_{dB}=h/\\sqrt{3mk_BT}\\)."),
    sub("c.2. Condensation estimate"),
    p("The volume per atom gives ℓ=n^(−1/3). Equating wavelengths gives \\(T_c=h^2n^{2/3}/(3mk_B)\\). This is the problem's estimate, not the exact ideal-Bose-gas transition coefficient."),
    sub("c.3. Critical density and comparison"),
    p("\\(n_c=(3mk_BT_c)^{3/2}/h^3\\simeq1.59\\times10^{18}\\,\\mathrm{m^{-3}}\\). The ideal gas law gives \\(n_0=p_0/(k_BT_0)\\simeq2.41\\times10^{25}\\,\\mathrm{m^{-3}}\\). The ordinary gas is about 1.5×10^7 times denser."),
    sub("d.1. Three-beam lattice potential"),
    p("Use 〈cos a cos b〉=cos(a−b)/2. Then \\[V=-\\alpha E_0^2[3/2+\\cos(\\mathbf b_1\\cdot\\mathbf r)+\\cos(\\mathbf b_2\\cdot\\mathbf r)+\\cos(\\mathbf b_3\\cdot\\mathbf r)],\\] with b1=k2−k3=k(0,√3), b2=k3−k1=k(−3/2,−√3/2), b3=k1−k2=k(3/2,−√3/2)."),
    sub("d.2. Sixfold symmetry"),
    p("A 60° rotation permutes the set of six reciprocal vectors ±b1,±b2,±b3. Since cosine is even, changing a vector to its negative does not alter its term. The sum and hence V are invariant."),
    sub("d.3. Axis profiles and extrema"),
    p("\\[V_X=-\\alpha E_0^2[5/2+2\\cos(3kx/2)],\\quad V_Y=-\\alpha E_0^2[3/2+\\cos2\\phi+2\\cos\\phi],\\quad\\phi=\\sqrt3ky/2.\\] VX has minima at x=0 and maxima at x=2π/(3k), modulo 4π/(3k). For VY, solve sin2φ+sinφ=0. In 0≤φ<2π, minima occur at φ=0 (deep) and π (shallow), maxima at 2π/3 and 4π/3. The φ=π minimum is only a one-dimensional minimum."),
    sub("d.4. True lattice minima"),
    p("The point x=0, φ=π is a minimum along y but a maximum along x, so is a saddle. The nearest true minima are \\((a,0),(a/2,\\sqrt3a/2),(-a/2,\\sqrt3a/2),(-a,0),(-a/2,-\\sqrt3a/2),(a/2,-\\sqrt3a/2)\\). Here \\(a=4\\pi/(3k)=2\\lambda_{las}/3\\)."),
    sub("d.5. Rydberg quantum number"),
    p("Equate Coulomb and centripetal forces and impose merv=nħ. Then \\(r=4\\pi\\epsilon_0n^2\\hbar^2/(m_ee^2)\\), so \\[n=\\frac e\\hbar\\sqrt{\\frac{m_e\\lambda_{las}}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0}}\\simeq85.\\]"),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2009-t1"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Rolling Cylinders"),
    p("A thin-walled cylinder of mass M and inner radius R rotates about a fixed horizontal axis OZ. A smaller uniform solid cylinder of mass m and radius r<R rolls inside it, with parallel axes and moment of inertia Icm=mr²/2. Contact is rough and rolling has no slip except for the short initial skid in 1.8. Gravity is g. Measure θ, the angle of the small centre from the downward vertical, and φ, the outer-cylinder rotation, in the same positive counterclockwise sense. ψ is the small cylinder's absolute spin angle. Initially both angles are zero and the small cylinder is at the bottom. Use \\(I_{cm}=mr^2/2\\), and let \\(\\theta=0\\) denote the lowest centre position."),
    fig("batch7-rolling-cylinders", "Original outer cylinder M of radius R and inner solid cylinder m of radius r, with axes and angular position theta."),
    sub("1.1. Rolling constraint"),
    p("Find ψ in terms of θ,φ,R,r."),
    sub("1.2. Spin acceleration"),
    p("Find the angular acceleration of the small cylinder in terms of the angular accelerations of θ and φ."),
    sub("1.3. Driven centre equation"),
    p("Derive an equation for θdouble-dot with general Icm."),
    sub("1.4. Period with fixed rotation rate"),
    p("If the outer cylinder is constrained to rotate at constant angular speed, find the small-oscillation period."),
    sub("1.5. Steady-rotation equilibrium"),
    p("Find the stable equilibrium angle for 1.4."),
    sub("1.6. Accelerating-shell equilibrium"),
    p("For constant outer angular acceleration α, find the stable equilibrium angle and its existence condition."),
    sub("1.7. Freely rotating outer cylinder"),
    p("Release the outer cylinder so it rotates freely during small oscillations. Derive the oscillation period."),
    sub("1.8. Abrupt stop and full ascent"),
    p("The shell initially rotates steadily at Ω while the small centre is stationary at the bottom. Stop and hold the shell abruptly. After a very short skid, the small cylinder rolls without slip. In the high-friction, locally-flat approximation, find the minimum Ω needed to reach the top while maintaining contact."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Rolling Cylinders"),
    sub("1.1. Rolling constraint"),
    p("Contact velocities obey \\((R-r)\\dot\\theta+r\\dot\\psi=R\\dot\\phi\\). Integrate with the stated initial angles: \\[\\psi=R\\phi/r-(R-r)\\theta/r.\\]"),
    sub("1.2. Spin acceleration"),
    p("Differentiate twice: \\[\\ddot\\psi=\\frac Rr\\ddot\\phi-\\frac{R-r}{r}\\ddot\\theta.\\]"),
    sub("1.3. Driven centre equation"),
    p("Tangential translation gives \\(m(R-r)\\ddot\\theta=f-mg\\sin\\theta\\). Spin torque gives \\(I_{cm}\\ddot\\psi=fr\\). Eliminating f using 1.2 yields \\[(m+I_{cm}/r^2)(R-r)\\ddot\\theta=-mg\\sin\\theta+(I_{cm}R/r^2)\\ddot\\phi.\\]"),
    sub("1.4. Period with fixed rotation rate"),
    p("Put φdouble-dot=0 and Icm=mr²/2. Then \\(\\ddot\\theta=-2g\\theta/[3(R-r)]\\), giving \\[T=2\\pi\\sqrt{3(R-r)/(2g)}.\\]"),
    sub("1.5. Steady-rotation equilibrium"),
    p("With no angular acceleration, the gravitational tangential force must vanish. The stable bottom equilibrium is θ=0."),
    sub("1.6. Accelerating-shell equilibrium"),
    p("At equilibrium \\(g\\sin\\theta_{eq}=R\\alpha/2\\), so \\(\\theta_{eq}=\\arcsin(R\\alpha/(2g))\\) on the branch with cosθ>0. It exists for |Rα|≤2g, with marginal restoration at equality."),
    sub("1.7. Freely rotating outer cylinder"),
    p("Its torque equation is \\(MR^2\\ddot\\phi=-fR\\). Combining this with the rolling constraint and the small-cylinder equations gives \\[\\ddot\\theta=-\\frac{g(2M+m)}{(R-r)(3M+m)}\\sin\\theta.\\] Thus \\[T=2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{(R-r)(3M+m)}{g(2M+m)}}.\\] The M→∞ limit agrees with 1.4."),
    sub("1.8. Abrupt stop and full ascent"),
    p("Before stopping, spin speed is RΩ/r. A friction impulse J gives mv=J and \\(I(\\omega-R\\Omega/r)=-Jr\\). At rolling onset v=rω, so v0=RΩ/3. Thereafter energy conservation gives top speed \\(v_t^2=v_0^2-8g(R-r)/3\\). The contact condition at the top is \\(v_t^2\\ge g(R-r)\\). Thus \\[\\Omega_{min}=\\frac{\\sqrt{33g(R-r)}}R.\\] Energy is lost during the skid and cannot be conserved across the abrupt-stop stage."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2013-t3"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Physics of Spin"),
    p("Use the classical analogue \\(\\dot{\\mathbf L}=\\boldsymbol\\mu\\times\\mathbf B\\), \\(\\boldsymbol\\mu=-\\gamma\\mathbf L\\), with γ>0. All frequencies in this problem are angular frequencies. A frame S′ rotates about z at +ω, with axes coincident with laboratory axes at t=0. The derivative rule is \\((d\\mathbf A/dt)_{lab}=(d\\mathbf A/dt)_{rot}+\\omega\\hat z\\times\\mathbf A\\). In part C an ensemble has Nup+Ndown=N and axial magnetization M=(Nup−Ndown)μ=Nμz. Initially all moments point along +z. The later measurement thought experiment uses its stated uncertainty estimate Δpx Δx≳ħ and its convention |μx|=|μz|=γħ."),
    sub("a.1. Conserved moment and angle"),
    p("Prove that |μ| is constant for any magnetic field. For stationary B show that its angle with μ is constant."),
    sub("a.2. Larmor frequency"),
    p("For B=B0 zhat find ω0 and its precession sense."),
    sub("b.1. Effective rotating-frame field"),
    p("Show that \\((d\\boldsymbol\\mu/dt)_{rot}=-\\gamma\\boldsymbol\\mu\\times\\mathbf B_{eff}\\), and find Beff."),
    sub("b.2. Detuning in rotating frame"),
    p("For static B=B0 zhat find the signed rotating-frame precession Δ in terms of ω0 and ω."),
    sub("b.3. Co-rotating applied field"),
    p("Add b(t)=b[cos(ωt)xhat+sin(ωt)yhat]. Derive the rotating-frame precession speed Ω."),
    sub("b.4. Counter-rotating field"),
    p("Instead add b[cos(ωt)xhat−sin(ωt)yhat]. Find Beff in S′ and its time average."),
    sub("c.1. Rotating-wave approximation"),
    p("Apply B=B0 zhat+2b cos(ω0t)xhat, with b≪B0 and ω=ω0=γB0. Show the approximate effective field and find Ω."),
    sub("c.2. Magnetization oscillation"),
    p("Find the angle α between the moment and the resonant effective field and prove M(t)=Nμ cos(Ωt)."),
    sub("c.3. Rabi populations"),
    p("Find and plot Pup(t),Pdown(t) on the same axes."),
    sub("d.1. Resolving the spin beams"),
    fig("batch7-spin-measurement", "Original oven, z-spin preparation region, selecting screen and second x-biased Stern-Gerlach region."),
    p("A silver beam travels along −y. A first z-biased Stern-Gerlach region and screen SC1 at distance D prepare +z moments. The screen opening has width Δx. The next x-biased region exerts Fx=μx C, with μx=±γħ. If the flight time after SC1 is t, show the condition needed to distinguish the two x beams."),
    sub("d.2. Loss of axial-spin information"),
    p("The second region has Bx=B0+Cx. Show that resolving μx causes a large spread in precession angle and destroys the prepared μz information in this model."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Physics of Spin"),
    sub("a.1. Conserved moment and angle"),
    p("\\(\\dot{\\boldsymbol\\mu}=-\\gamma\\boldsymbol\\mu\\times\\mathbf B\\). Therefore \\(d\\mu^2/dt=2\\boldsymbol\\mu\\cdot\\dot{\\boldsymbol\\mu}=0\\). If B is fixed, \\(d(\\boldsymbol\\mu\\cdot\\mathbf B)/dt=0\\) also. Both magnitudes and their dot product are fixed, so the angle is fixed."),
    sub("a.2. Larmor frequency"),
    p("\\(\\dot{\\boldsymbol\\mu}=\\gamma\\mathbf B\\times\\boldsymbol\\mu\\), so the angular velocity vector is +γB0 zhat and \\(\\omega_0=\\gamma B_0\\)."),
    sub("b.1. Effective rotating-frame field"),
    p("Subtract frame rotation from the lab derivative. Since \\(-\\omega\\hat z\\times\\boldsymbol\\mu=\\omega\\boldsymbol\\mu\\times\\hat z\\), the effective field is \\(\\mathbf B_{eff}=\\mathbf B-(\\omega/\\gamma)\\hat z^{\\prime}\\)."),
    sub("b.2. Detuning in rotating frame"),
    p("The signed axial angular velocity is \\(\\Delta=\\omega_0-\\omega\\). Its nonnegative speed is |Δ|. The sign records the direction of relative rotation."),
    sub("b.3. Co-rotating applied field"),
    p("The rotating field is constant along x′. Thus \\(\\mathbf B_{eff}=b\\hat x^{\\prime}+(B_0-\\omega/\\gamma)\\hat z^{\\prime}\\) and \\[\\Omega=\\gamma\\sqrt{(B_0-\\omega/\\gamma)^2+b^2}.\\]"),
    sub("b.4. Counter-rotating field"),
    p("The field rotates at −2ω in S′: \\[\\mathbf B_{eff}=(B_0-\\omega/\\gamma)\\hat z^{\\prime}+b[\\cos2\\omega t\\,\\hat x^{\\prime}-\\sin2\\omega t\\,\\hat y^{\\prime}].\\] Averaging a full rotation leaves only \\((B_0-\\omega/\\gamma)\\hat z^{\\prime}\\)."),
    sub("c.1. Rotating-wave approximation"),
    p("Split the linear oscillation into equal co- and counter-rotating fields. On resonance the static axial effective field cancels. The counter-rotating piece oscillates at 2ω0, much faster than γb, so its leading average vanishes. Thus \\(\\mathbf B_{eff}\\simeq b\\hat x^{\\prime}\\), \\(\\Omega=\\gamma b\\)."),
    sub("c.2. Magnetization oscillation"),
    p("Initially μ is along z and Beff is along x′, so α=π/2 and remains fixed. Rotation about x′ gives \\(\\mu_z=\\mu\\cos\\Omega t\\). Since z′=z, the measured axial magnetization is \\(M=N\\mu\\cos\\Omega t\\)."),
    sub("c.3. Rabi populations"),
    p("Solve Pup+Pdown=1 and Pup−Pdown=M/(Nμ): \\[P_{up}=\\cos^2(\\Omega t/2),\\qquad P_{down}=\\sin^2(\\Omega t/2).\\] They are complementary, have period 2π/Ω, and exchange populations at t=π/Ω. At t=0 Pup=1."),
    sub("d.1. Resolving the spin beams"),
    p("Uncertainty gives transverse velocity spread \\(\\Delta v_x\\gtrsim\\hbar/(m\\Delta x)\\), so width grows by roughly ħt/(mΔx). Opposite forces separate the beam centres by \\(d_x=|\\mu_x|Ct^2/m\\). Resolution requires \\[|\\mu_x|C\\Delta x\\,t/\\hbar\\gg1.\\]"),
    sub("d.2. Loss of axial-spin information"),
    p("Field variation is ΔB=CΔx, giving \\(\\Delta\\omega=\\gamma C\\Delta x=(|\\mu_x|/\\hbar)C\\Delta x\\). The resolution condition therefore implies Δωt≫1. The ensemble acquires a broad spread of rotations around x, so its original definite z projection is lost. This gives the intended incompatibility argument under the stated semiclassical estimate."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2018-t1"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Optical trap of neutral atoms"),
    p("Model a neutral sodium atom as a massive +e core and a −e electron cloud. In a static field E0 uhat its induced moment is p0=αE0 uhat. In a laser field E(r,t)=E0(r)cos(ωt)uhat, write the moment as p=α(ω)E0(r)cos[ωt+φ(ω)]uhat. Use the positive-amplitude convention α≥0 and phase −π≤φ≤0, so a passive response lags the field. This is equivalent to the source's signed-amplitude convention. Intensity is I=ε0cE0²/2. The electron equation is \\(\\ddot x+\\gamma_\\omega\\dot x+\\omega_0^2x=-eE_0\\cos\\omega t/m_e\\), and p=−ex. Larmor power is \\(P_L=e^2a^2/(6\\pi\\epsilon_0c^3)\\). Use e=1.602×10^−19 C, me=9.109×10^−31 kg, ε0=8.854×10^−12 F/m, c=2.998×10^8 m/s, kB=1.381×10^−23 J/K and ħ=1.055×10^−34 J s."),
    fig("batch7-electron-cloud", "Original coincident positive core and negative cloud centres and their separation in an electric field."),
    sub("1.1. Power absorbed by induced dipole"),
    p("Increase a uniform field very slowly from zero. Find instantaneous absorbed power in E and pdot."),
    sub("1.2. Static induced potential"),
    p("Find total work in raising the field from zero to E0 and the induced potential energy."),
    sub("2.1. Mean optical potential"),
    p("Find the time-averaged induced potential in α,φ,ε0,c,I."),
    sub("3.1. Photon scattering rate"),
    p("The scattering rate is mean absorbed power divided by ħω. Find it."),
    sub("4.1. Oscillator polarizability"),
    p("Solve the driven oscillator for α and the phase relations in the stated positive-amplitude convention."),
    sub("5.1. Radiative damping"),
    p("An electron moves in a circular orbit of angular frequency ω and speed v. Equate Larmor radiation to the work of damping force −me γω v and find γω."),
    sub("6.1. Trap-to-scattering ratio"),
    p("Define γ=γω0. Find Udip/(ħΓsc) in terms of ω,ω0,γ."),
    sub("7.1. Gaussian trap depth"),
    fig("batch7-gaussian-trap", "Original Gaussian laser beam envelope with waist D0 and Rayleigh length zR beside the trapping potential and its harmonic approximation."),
    p("For ω<ω0 a Gaussian beam has \\(I(\\rho,z)=2P\\exp[-2\\rho^2/D(z)^2]/[\\pi D(z)^2]\\), \\(D(z)=D_0\\sqrt{1+z^2/z_R^2}\\), \\(z_R=\\pi D_0^2/\\lambda\\). Find the positive trap depth in c,ω,ω0,γ,P,D0."),
    sub("7.2. Equivalent trap temperature"),
    p("Use P=4 mW, λ=985 nm, D0=6 μm, sodium λ0=589 nm. Evaluate depth as temperature using the explicit convention Udepth=kBT0."),
    sub("8.1. Trap frequencies"),
    p("Expand the Gaussian potential near its bottom as \\(-U_{depth}+m\\Omega_\\rho^2\\rho^2/2+m\\Omega_z^2z^2/2\\). Find both frequencies using Udepth=kBT0."),
    sub("9.1. Condensate size estimate"),
    p("For U(z)=Umin+mΩz²z²/2 estimate size z0 by balancing potential energy against uncertainty kinetic energy. State your width convention."),
    sub("9.2. Lowest energy estimate"),
    p("Find the lowest energy scale and distinguish the uncertainty estimate from the exact oscillator ground-state value."),
    sub("9.3. Characteristic velocity"),
    p("Estimate a characteristic condensate speed along z."),
    sub("10.1. Initial condensate aspect ratio"),
    fig("batch7-cloud-inversion", "Original initial elongated trapped condensate and flattened condensate after a long free expansion."),
    p("Extend the oscillator estimate to 3D and find radial-to-axial size ratio. State whether radial width denotes one transverse axis or √〈x²+y²〉."),
    sub("10.2. Condensate expansion velocity ratio"),
    p("Find the radial-to-axial characteristic expansion-speed ratio using the same convention as 10.1."),
    sub("10.3. Late condensate aspect ratio"),
    p("Assume free expansion with constant characteristic velocities and final dimensions much larger than their initial values. Find the late aspect ratio."),
    sub("10.4. Late thermal-cloud shape"),
    p("Find the late thermal-cloud aspect ratio and compare with the condensate."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Optical trap of neutral atoms"),
    sub("1.1. Power absorbed by induced dipole"),
    p("The two charge forces do total work \\(dW=\\mathbf E\\cdot d\\mathbf p\\), giving \\(P_{abs}=\\mathbf E\\cdot\\dot{\\mathbf p}\\)."),
    sub("1.2. Static induced potential"),
    p("For p=αE, \\(W=\\int_0^{E_0}E\\alpha dE=\\alpha E_0^2/2=\\mathbf p_0\\cdot\\mathbf E_0/2\\). The induced mechanical trapping potential is \\(U_{ind}=-\\mathbf p_0\\cdot\\mathbf E_0/2\\). The factor one half accounts for inducing the dipole."),
    sub("2.1. Mean optical potential"),
    p("Average \\(-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf E/2\\): \\[U_{dip}=-\\alpha E_0^2\\cos\\phi/4=-\\frac{\\alpha I\\cos\\phi}{2\\epsilon_0c}.\\]"),
    sub("3.1. Photon scattering rate"),
    p("\\(\\langle\\mathbf E\\cdot\\dot{\\mathbf p}\\rangle=-\\alpha\\omega E_0^2\\sin\\phi/2\\). Thus \\[\\Gamma_{sc}=-\\frac{\\alpha I\\sin\\phi}{\\hbar\\epsilon_0c}.\\] With the lag convention sinφ≤0, this is nonnegative."),
    sub("4.1. Oscillator polarizability"),
    p("Put \\(D=\\sqrt{(\\omega_0^2-\\omega^2)^2+\\gamma_\\omega^2\\omega^2}\\). Then \\[\\alpha=\\frac{e^2}{m_eD},\\quad\\cos\\phi=\\frac{\\omega_0^2-\\omega^2}{D},\\quad\\sin\\phi=-\\frac{\\gamma_\\omega\\omega}{D}.\\] The source instead permits a negative α and shifts φ by π. The observable products αcosφ and αsinφ agree."),
    sub("5.1. Radiative damping"),
    p("Since a=ωv, \\(m_e\\gamma_\\omega v^2=e^2\\omega^2v^2/(6\\pi\\epsilon_0c^3)\\), giving \\(\\gamma_\\omega=e^2\\omega^2/(6\\pi\\epsilon_0m_ec^3)\\)."),
    sub("6.1. Trap-to-scattering ratio"),
    p("The ratio is \\(\\tfrac12\\cot\\phi=-(\\omega_0^2-\\omega^2)/(2\\gamma_\\omega\\omega)\\). With \\(\\gamma_\\omega=\\gamma(\\omega/\\omega_0)^2\\), \\[\\frac{U_{dip}}{\\hbar\\Gamma_{sc}}=-\\frac{\\omega_0^2(\\omega_0^2-\\omega^2)}{2\\gamma\\omega^3}.\\] It is negative for red detuning ω<ω0."),
    sub("7.1. Gaussian trap depth"),
    p("The deepest point is ρ=z=0. Replace e²/(ε0me) by 6πc³γ/ω0² to obtain \\[U_{depth}=\\frac{6c^2\\gamma P(\\omega_0^2-\\omega^2)}{\\omega_0^2D_0^2[(\\omega_0^2-\\omega^2)^2+\\gamma^2\\omega^6/\\omega_0^4]}.\\]"),
    sub("7.2. Equivalent trap temperature"),
    p("\\(\\omega=2\\pi c/\\lambda\\), \\(\\omega_0=2\\pi c/\\lambda_0\\), γ≈6.4×10^7 s^−1. These give Udepth/kB≈4.13 μK. The source also accepts thermal-energy conventions f kBT0, with f=1/2 or 3/2, but this field fixes f=1."),
    sub("8.1. Trap frequencies"),
    p("Expansion gives \\(U=-U_{depth}+2U_{depth}\\rho^2/D_0^2+U_{depth}z^2/z_R^2\\). Thus \\(\\Omega_\\rho=\\sqrt{4k_BT_0/(mD_0^2)}\\), \\(\\Omega_z=\\sqrt{2k_BT_0/(mz_R^2)}\\)."),
    sub("9.1. Condensate size estimate"),
    p("Using the source's estimate pz0≈ħ gives \\(E-U_{min}\\sim m\\Omega_z^2z_0^2/2+\\hbar^2/(2mz_0^2)\\), minimized at \\(z_0\\sim\\sqrt{\\hbar/(m\\Omega_z)}\\). If z0 means exact rms width, use Δz=√[ħ/(2mΩz)]. Numerical width factors depend on this choice."),
    sub("9.2. Lowest energy estimate"),
    p("The source's pz0≈ħ estimate yields \\(E_{min}-U_{min}\\sim\\hbar\\Omega_z\\). The exact quantum harmonic oscillator gives \\(E_{min}-U_{min}=\\hbar\\Omega_z/2\\). Both convey the requested scale and the source accepts the exact result."),
    sub("9.3. Characteristic velocity"),
    p("The uncertainty estimate gives \\(v_z\\sim\\hbar/(mz_0)\\sim\\sqrt{\\hbar\\Omega_z/m}\\). This is a typical or rms scale, not signed mean velocity, which vanishes. The exact rms speed is √[ħΩz/(2m)]."),
    sub("10.1. Initial condensate aspect ratio"),
    p("For per-axis widths, \\(x_0/z_0=\\sqrt{\\Omega_z/\\Omega_\\rho}\\). If \\(\\rho_0=\\sqrt{x_0^2+y_0^2}\\), then \\(\\rho_0/z_0=\\sqrt{2\\Omega_z/\\Omega_\\rho}\\). The initially tighter radial confinement makes a cigar-shaped cloud. The source accepts either explicit width convention."),
    sub("10.2. Condensate expansion velocity ratio"),
    p("For one transverse component, \\(v_x/v_z=\\sqrt{\\Omega_\\rho/\\Omega_z}\\). For combined radial rms speed, \\(v_\\rho/v_z=\\sqrt{2\\Omega_\\rho/\\Omega_z}\\). Confinement and velocity width vary oppositely."),
    sub("10.3. Late condensate aspect ratio"),
    p("The late width ratio is the velocity ratio. Per-axis \\(x_L/z_L\\to\\sqrt{\\Omega_\\rho/\\Omega_z}\\), or combined radial \\(\\rho_L/z_L\\to\\sqrt{2\\Omega_\\rho/\\Omega_z}\\). The condensate expands faster in the initially tightly confined direction, reversing the visible anisotropy."),
    sub("10.4. Late thermal-cloud shape"),
    p("Maxwell velocities have equal Cartesian rms components. Thus late per-axis widths satisfy xL/zL=1 and the cloud is spherical, unlike the condensate. A combined radial rms width √〈x²+y²〉 divided by axial rms width is √2 even for a sphere. This geometric convention must not be confused with visible anisotropy. The source's quoted ratio 1 refers to equal widths along individual directions."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2019-t1"] = {
  statement: [
    h("RF reflectometry for spin readout for silicon quantum computing"),
    p("Model lossless TEM transmission lines and ideal circuit elements. A coaxial line has inner-core radius a and shield inner radius b, filled with relative permittivity εr and permeability μr. Ignore conductor resistance, internal inductance and dielectric leakage. Use ε=ε0εr, μ=μ0μr, c0=1/√(ε0μ0), with ε0=8.854×10^−12 F/m and μ0=4π×10^−7 H/m. Reflection coefficients are voltage-amplitude ratios, including phase, not power ratios. For phasors use exp(iωt), so a capacitor has impedance 1/(iωC). The SET and SLQD circuit diagrams specify which elements are in series and parallel. Use \\(\\epsilon=\\epsilon_0\\epsilon_r\\), \\(\\mu=\\mu_0\\mu_r\\), and \\(\\Gamma=V_r/V_i\\)."),
    sub("a.1. Wave speed"),
    p("Find the electromagnetic propagation speed in the cable."),
    sub("a.2. Coaxial electric field"),
    p("A length Δx of core carries charge Δq and the outer shield is grounded. Find E(r) between the conductors."),
    sub("a.3. Capacitance per length"),
    p("Find Cx."),
    sub("a.4. Inductance per length"),
    p("Find Lx."),
    sub("a.5. Characteristic impedance"),
    fig("batch7-coax-ladder", "Original lumped coaxial transmission-line ladder with series inductances and shunt capacitances."),
    p("Use the short-section ladder to show Z0=√(Lx/Cx). For Z0=50 Ω, εr=4, μr=1 calculate b/a."),
    sub("b.1. Wire above ground plane"),
    fig("batch7-wire-plane", "Original thin signal conductor of radius a at height d above its grounded return plane."),
    p("A thin wire of radius a lies distance d≫a from an infinite grounded plane in the same medium. Find its characteristic impedance."),
    sub("c.1. Voltage reflection coefficient"),
    p("A line of real impedance Z0 joins a line of impedance Z1. Derive Γ=Vr/Vi."),
    sub("c.2. Reflection phase inversion"),
    p("For positive real impedances, when does reflection introduce a π phase shift?"),
    sub("d.1. Quantum-dot potential and addition energy"),
    fig("batch7-set-circuit", "Original single-electron transistor showing source, drain, quantum dot, tunnel junctions and gate capacitance."),
    p("A quantum dot has n excess electrons, total capacitance Cg dominated by its gate at voltage Vg. Source and drain are grounded and tunnel capacitances Ct≪Cg. Find φn and the energy to add one electron."),
    sub("d.2. Equilibrium addition-energy scale"),
    p("Electrons enter until the smallest integer N with ΔEN≥0 is reached. Find the maximum achievable equilibrium addition-energy scale Ec as Vg varies."),
    sub("d.3. Thermal condition"),
    p("Give the temperature condition for a strong distinction between ON and OFF states."),
    sub("d.4. Tunnelling time and resistance"),
    p("Estimate the tunnel-junction time from Rt,Ct. Using the source's lifetime broadening h/τ, find the resistance condition to preserve distinct addition energies."),
    sub("e.1. Bare SET reflection contrast"),
    p("The SET is Ron=100 kΩ when ON and open-circuit when OFF, connected directly to Z0=50 Ω. Find ΔΓ=|Γon−Γoff|."),
    sub("e.2. Series-inductor matching circuit"),
    fig("batch7-set-matching", "Original series L0 feeding the parallel combination of parasitic C0 and SET impedance."),
    p("Add series L0 before the parallel combination of C0=0.4 pF and the SET. At f=100 MHz estimate L0 for order-one ΔΓ, and compute the resulting contrast."),
    sub("f.1. Single-lead quantum-dot readout"),
    fig("batch7-slqd-matching", "Original parallel inductor L0, parasitic C0 and single-lead quantum-dot impedance at the end of the transmission line."),
    p("In the SLQD circuit L0,C0 and the dot are all in parallel. The dot is open when OFF and a capacitance Cq when ON. With C0 fixed, suggest ωrf and ZC=√(L0/C0) for ΔΓ of order one."),
    sub("f.2. Additional matching element"),
    p("If the fixed inductance gives ZC much smaller than Z0Cq/C0, draw where to add one element and specify its value to retain ΔΓ of order one."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("RF reflectometry for spin readout for silicon quantum computing"),
    sub("a.1. Wave speed"),
    p("TEM propagation speed is \\(v=1/\\sqrt{\\epsilon\\mu}=c_0/\\sqrt{\\epsilon_r\\mu_r}\\)."),
    sub("a.2. Coaxial electric field"),
    p("Gauss's law on a cylindrical surface gives \\(E(r)=\\Delta q/(2\\pi\\epsilon\\Delta x\\,r)\\), radially outward for positive charge."),
    sub("a.3. Capacitance per length"),
    p("The core potential is \\(\\Delta q\\ln(b/a)/(2\\pi\\epsilon\\Delta x)\\). Hence \\(C_x=2\\pi\\epsilon/\\ln(b/a)\\)."),
    sub("a.4. Inductance per length"),
    p("Between the conductors \\(B(r)=\\mu I/(2\\pi r)\\). Integrating flux per unit length gives \\(L_x=\\mu\\ln(b/a)/(2\\pi)\\)."),
    sub("a.5. Characteristic impedance"),
    p("Appending a short cell with series iωLx dx and shunt iωCx dx must leave the semi-infinite impedance unchanged. To first order this gives Lx−CxZ0²=0. Thus \\(Z_0=\\ln(b/a)\\sqrt{\\mu/\\epsilon}/(2\\pi)\\). Since √(μ0/ε0)/(2π)=59.96 Ω, \\(b/a=\\exp[50\\sqrt4/59.96]\\simeq5.30\\)."),
    sub("b.1. Wire above ground plane"),
    p("Replace the plane by opposite charge and opposite return current in an image wire 2d away. To leading order \\(C_x=2\\pi\\epsilon/\\ln(2d/a)\\), \\(L_x=\\mu\\ln(2d/a)/(2\\pi)\\). Therefore \\[Z_0=\\frac{\\ln(2d/a)}{2\\pi}\\sqrt{\\frac\\mu\\epsilon}.\\]"),
    sub("c.1. Voltage reflection coefficient"),
    p("Voltage continuity gives Vi+Vr=Vt, current continuity gives (Vi−Vr)/Z0=Vt/Z1. Thus \\(\\Gamma=(Z_1-Z_0)/(Z_1+Z_0)\\). The reflected current has the opposite propagation direction."),
    sub("c.2. Reflection phase inversion"),
    p("A π shift means Γ<0, occurring for Z1<Z0. At Z1=Z0 reflection vanishes, so a reflected phase is not defined."),
    sub("d.1. Quantum-dot potential and addition energy"),
    p("With electron charge −e, \\(\\phi_n=V_g-ne/C_g\\). Integrate the potential while adding charge −e, or average the initial and final potentials: \\[\\Delta E_n=-e(\\phi_n+\\phi_{n+1})/2=\\frac{e^2}{C_g}(n+1/2)-eV_g.\\] The voltage source's work must be included."),
    sub("d.2. Equilibrium addition-energy scale"),
    p("Addition energies are spaced by e²/Cg. As a gate threshold is crossed, N increments and ΔEN resets from approximately zero to approximately e²/Cg. Thus \\(E_c=e^2/C_g\\), understood as the supremum of this sawtooth if exact degeneracy is assigned to the lower-N state."),
    sub("d.3. Thermal condition"),
    p("Thermal excitations must be much smaller than the addition energy: \\(k_BT\\ll E_c=e^2/C_g\\). Near a particular gate degeneracy the actual ΔEN, rather than its maximum, sets the stricter condition."),
    sub("d.4. Tunnelling time and resistance"),
    p("\\(\\tau\\sim R_tC_t\\). Requiring \\(h/(R_tC_t)\\ll e^2/C_g\\) gives \\(R_t\\gg hC_g/(e^2C_t)\\), which is stronger than h/e² because Cg≫Ct. This uses the specifically requested junction-capacitance time estimate."),
    sub("e.1. Bare SET reflection contrast"),
    p("Γoff=1 and Γon=(100000−50)/(100000+50). Thus \\(\\Delta\\Gamma=100/100050\\simeq9.995\\times10^{-4}\\)."),
    sub("e.2. Series-inductor matching circuit"),
    p("Tune the OFF series LC resonance: \\(L_0=1/(\\omega^2C_0)=6.33\\,\\mu H\\), giving Zoff=0 and Γoff=−1. In the ON state \\(Z_{on}=i\\omega L_0+[1/R_{on}+i\\omega C_0]^{-1}\\simeq(158+6.3i)\\,\\Omega\\). Thus Γon≈0.520+0.0145i and ΔΓ≈1.52. The contrast can exceed one because it is a difference of complex amplitudes."),
    sub("f.1. Single-lead quantum-dot readout"),
    p("Tune \\(\\omega_{rf}=1/\\sqrt{L_0C_0}\\), so the OFF admittance vanishes and Γoff=1. ON impedance is \\(Z_{on}=1/(i\\omega_{rf}C_q)=-iZ_CC_0/C_q\\). Make its magnitude comparable to Z0: \\(Z_C\\sim Z_0C_q/C_0\\). At equality Γon=−i and ΔΓ=√2 in the stated phasor convention."),
    sub("f.2. Additional matching element"),
    p("Add a capacitor Cm in series between the transmission line and the entire parallel LC-dot network. At the same OFF resonance the network remains open, so Γoff=1. ON impedance is \\(-i[1/(\\omega C_q)+1/(\\omega C_m)]\\). Set its magnitude to Z0: \\[C_m=\\frac{C_q}{Z_0\\omega C_q-1}\\simeq\\frac1{Z_0\\omega}.\\] The assumed inequality ensures Cm>0. Then Γon=−i and ΔΓ=√2. The source's +i uses the opposite phasor sign and does not change the contrast."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2019-t2"] = {
  statement: [
    h("X-ray jets from active galactic nuclei"),
    p("Use the historical one-dimensional steady jet model. Distance s is measured from the active galactic nucleus (AGN), cross-section A=πr², speed v, Lorentz factor γ, and proper particle density n. Material is injected at rest in the AGN frame at uniform mass rate α per volume. Mean rest mass per particle is μpp=0.59mp, and the supplied effective energy/enthalpy per particle is εav=μpp c²+13P/(4n). Treat its energy-flux prescription as the given model. For Centaurus A take jet power Pj=10^36 W, s1=252 pc, r1=30 pc, v1=0.667c, s2=5.94 kpc, r2=500 pc, v2=0.52c, and a straight conical boundary between these ends. External pressure is P(s)=5.7×10^−12(s/1 kpc)^−1.5 Pa. Use 1 pc=3.086×10^16 m and c=3.00×10^8 m/s. Later sections use an isotropic ultrarelativistic electron gas with proper differential density f(ε)dε and positive electron charge magnitude e. Source discrepancies are explained in the worked solution rather than silently enforced as grading keys."),
    fig("batch7-jet-geometry", "Original conical Centaurus A jet with labelled inlet and outlet distances, radii and bulk velocities."),
    sub("a.1. AGN-frame density"),
    p("Find nprime from proper density n."),
    sub("a.2. Particle flux"),
    p("Find the number of particles crossing a jet section per time."),
    sub("a.3. Mass-loading continuity"),
    p("For total segment volume V, relate incoming and outgoing particle fluxes."),
    sub("a.4. Energy balance"),
    p("Using the supplied model, write the energy-flux relation between s1 and s2."),
    sub("a.5. Jet-power derivative"),
    p("Define \\(P_j=F_E-\\dot Mc^2\\), with mass flux \\(\\dot M=\\mu_{pp}F_p\\). Find dPj/ds."),
    sub("a.6. End mass fluxes"),
    p("Find a formula for mass flux from Pj,γ,A,v,P and evaluate it at both ends."),
    sub("a.7. Momentum flux increase"),
    p("Find the model axial momentum flux at a section and its net increase through the segment. Evaluate it."),
    sub("a.8. External pressure force"),
    p("Find the total axial external-pressure force on the conical jet segment."),
    sub("a.9. Momentum-balance discrepancy"),
    p("Write the expected momentum balance and calculate the percentage discrepancy relative to the expected pressure force, |Π−FPr|/|FPr|×100%."),
    sub("b.1. Electron energy density"),
    p("Give the total energy per volume for differential number density f(ε)."),
    sub("b.2. Wall momentum transfer"),
    p("For an isotropic ultrarelativistic gas, derive the magnitude of normal momentum delivered per time to an elastic wall area ΔA."),
    sub("b.3. Ultrarelativistic equation of state"),
    p("Relate pressure, volume and total internal energy."),
    sub("b.4. Adiabatic expansion law"),
    p("Derive P(V) for adiabatic expansion."),
    sub("c.1. Gyration angular frequency"),
    p("An electron of Lorentz factor γ moves at pitch angle φ to a uniform B. Find its gyrofrequency about B, distinguishing it from the angular rate at which its velocity direction sweeps across an observer."),
    sub("c.2. Observed synchrotron pulse duration"),
    fig("batch7-relativistic-beaming", "Original electron radiation patterns in the instantaneous rest frame and observer frame, with acceleration and velocity directions."),
    p("Radiation is beamed into half-angle about 1/γ. Estimate the duration seen by an observer near the instantaneous velocity direction. Retain pitch-angle dependence, assuming sinφ is not extremely small."),
    sub("c.3. Characteristic radiation frequency"),
    p("Estimate νchr from the observed pulse width."),
    sub("c.4. Synchrotron cooling time"),
    p("Given \\(P_s=e^4B^2E^2\\sin^2\\phi/(6\\pi\\epsilon_0m^4c^5)\\), estimate the energy-loss timescale."),
    sub("d.1. Frozen-in magnetic field"),
    p("A spherical knot expands isotropically from V0 to V with frozen magnetic flux. Find B(V)."),
    sub("d.2. Expanded energy distribution"),
    p("Initially f0(ε)=κ0 ε^−p over finite physical energy bounds. Find f(ε) after adiabatic expansion to V. Account for both dilution and shifted particle energies."),
    sub("d.3. Cooling distortion"),
    p("Compare the evolution of electrons with ε1<ε2 under synchrotron cooling. Does the high-energy spectrum become steeper? State the assumptions behind that interpretation."),
    sub("d.4. Interpret knot fading"),
    p("Given observations: Cen A AX1C and BX2 fade by 23% and 15% over 15 years with no X-ray spectral change. M87 HST-1 and Knot A fade by 73% and 12% over 5 years with unchanged brightness in lower-frequency bands and no X-ray spectral data. Choose the more likely of adiabatic expansion and synchrotron cooling for each, citing previous reasoning. Use \\(j(\\nu)\\propto B^{(1+p)/2}\\nu^{(1-p)/2}\\)."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("X-ray jets from active galactic nuclei"),
    sub("a.1. AGN-frame density"),
    p("Longitudinal lengths contract by 1/γ while particle number and transverse area are invariant. Thus \\(n^{\\prime}=\\gamma n\\)."),
    sub("a.2. Particle flux"),
    p("The swept volume per time is Av, so \\(F_p=\\gamma nAv\\)."),
    sub("a.3. Mass-loading continuity"),
    p("Injected particle rate is αV/μpp, hence \\(\\gamma_2n_2A_2v_2-\\gamma_1n_1A_1v_1=\\alpha V/\\mu_{pp}\\)."),
    sub("a.4. Energy balance"),
    p("Each particle's model energy transforms by γ, while the number flux has another γ. Thus \\(F_E=\\gamma^2nAv\\epsilon_{av}\\). Material injected at rest adds only rest energy, so \\(F_E(s_2)-F_E(s_1)=\\alpha Vc^2\\)."),
    sub("a.5. Jet-power derivative"),
    p("Energy injection is αVc² and rest-mass flux increases by αV. Their contributions cancel in Pj for any segment. Thus \\(dP_j/ds=0\\)."),
    sub("a.6. End mass fluxes"),
    p("Rearrange \\(P_j=(\\gamma-1)\\dot Mc^2+(13/4)\\gamma^2AvP\\): \\[\\dot M=\\frac{P_j-(13/4)\\gamma^2AvP}{(\\gamma-1)c^2}.\\] Substitution gives approximately 2.8×10^19 kg/s and 5.2×10^19 kg/s at the inlet and outlet. The displayed inputs support only about two significant figures."),
    sub("a.7. Momentum flux increase"),
    p("The model momentum flux is \\(\\Pi(s)=F_E(s)v(s)/c^2=[P_j+\\dot M(s)c^2]v(s)/c^2\\). The net increase is Π2−Π1, approximately 2.01×10^27 N with the supplied numerical inputs. The source quotes 1.9×10^27 N after rounding. The phrase total momentum flux in this task refers to this difference, not either endpoint alone."),
    sub("a.8. External pressure force"),
    p("The end forces are P1A1−P2A2. The sloping wall contributes an outward axial component, giving \\[F_{Pr}=P_1A_1-P_2A_2+\\int_{r_1}^{r_2}2\\pi rP[s(r)]\\,dr,\\quad s(r)=s_1+\\frac{s_2-s_1}{r_2-r_1}(r-r_1).\\] The integral is about 9.71×10^26 N and the net force about 8.10×10^26 N with the supplied inputs. The source rounds these to 9.8×10^26 N and 8.2×10^26 N."),
    sub("a.9. Momentum-balance discrepancy"),
    p("The model would require Π=FPr. With the quoted rounded results, \\(|1.9-0.82|/0.82\\times100\\%\\simeq132\\%\\). Direct calculation before rounding gives about 148% from Π≈2.01×10^27 N and FPr≈8.10×10^26 N. The source prints 40%, inconsistent with both its stated denominator and its displayed numbers. Use the defined denominator and show your arithmetic."),
    sub("b.1. Electron energy density"),
    p("\\[u=U/V=\\int_0^\\infty\\epsilon f(\\epsilon)\\,d\\epsilon.\\] Physical distributions must have cutoffs or decay sufficiently rapidly for this integral to exist."),
    sub("b.2. Wall momentum transfer"),
    p("A particle incident at θ carries normal momentum (ε/c)cosθ and reverses it. The incident flux includes c cosθ and solid-angle weight sinθ dθdφ/(4π). Integrating the incident hemisphere gives \\[\\frac{\\Delta p_z}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{\\Delta A}{3}\\int_0^\\infty\\epsilon f(\\epsilon)d\\epsilon.\\] The gas momentum change is opposite to the force on the wall."),
    sub("b.3. Ultrarelativistic equation of state"),
    p("Divide the wall force by area to obtain P=u/3, or \\(U=3PV\\)."),
    sub("b.4. Adiabatic expansion law"),
    p("With no heat input, dU=−P dV. Since U=3PV, \\(3VdP+4PdV=0\\). Integrate to get \\(PV^{4/3}=\\text{constant}\\)."),
    sub("c.1. Gyration angular frequency"),
    p("The perpendicular speed is v sinφ. Force balance is \\(eBv\\sin\\phi=\\gamma m\\Omega_gv\\sin\\phi\\), giving \\(\\Omega_g=eB/(\\gamma m)\\), independent of nonzero pitch. The speed of the unit velocity vector on the direction sphere is \\(\\Omega_g\\sin\\phi\\). The source calls the latter Ω but labels it gyration frequency. The distinction is needed to retain the correct pitch dependence."),
    sub("c.2. Observed synchrotron pulse duration"),
    p("The emission direction traverses an angular width 2/γ at rate Ωg sinφ, so emission duration is \\(2/(\\gamma\\Omega_g\\sin\\phi)\\). Arrival-time compression is of order 1−v/c≈1/(2γ²). Thus \\[\\Delta t_{obs}\\sim\\frac{m}{\\gamma^2eB\\sin\\phi}.\\] Numerical cone factors are approximate. The source's pitch-independent result corresponds to sinφ≈1 and should not be applied at arbitrary pitch."),
    sub("c.3. Characteristic radiation frequency"),
    p("\\[\\nu_{chr}\\sim1/\\Delta t_{obs}\\sim\\gamma^2eB\\sin\\phi/m.\\] This is a characteristic inverse width, not the gyrofrequency in cycles per second. A precise spectral-peak calculation supplies additional numerical factors."),
    sub("c.4. Synchrotron cooling time"),
    p("\\(\\tau=E/P_s=6\\pi\\epsilon_0m^4c^5/[e^4B^2E\\sin^2\\phi]\\). It is an instantaneous loss timescale, decreasing with energy."),
    sub("d.1. Frozen-in magnetic field"),
    p("Linear dimensions scale as (V/V0)^(1/3), and any material area as (V/V0)^(2/3). Conserved flux gives \\(B=B_0(V/V_0)^{-2/3}\\). The relevant flux is through a material cross-section, not the identically zero net flux through a closed surface."),
    sub("d.2. Expanded energy distribution"),
    p("Let a=V/V0. From U∝V^(−1/3), each energy scales as ε=ε0 a^(−1/3). Conservation in an energy bin gives \\(Vf(\\epsilon)d\\epsilon=V_0f_0(\\epsilon_0)d\\epsilon_0\\). Therefore \\[f(\\epsilon)=\\kappa_0a^{-(p+2)/3}\\epsilon^{-p}.\\] The energy bounds scale by a^(−1/3). The source's a^−1 normalization omits the energy-bin Jacobian and energy shift, and integrates an unbounded power law that need not converge. The slope stays p but its normalization is not simple number dilution."),
    sub("d.3. Cooling distortion"),
    p("For fixed field and pitch, \\(\\dot\\epsilon=-a_s\\epsilon^2\\), so \\(\\epsilon(t)=\\epsilon_i/(1+a_s\\epsilon_it)\\) and loss time is 1/(as εi). Higher energies cool faster. An initially finite power-law population develops a high-energy cutoff or break rather than preserving its original spectrum. For an impulsive population, \\(f(\\epsilon,t)=\\kappa\\epsilon^{-p}(1-a_st\\epsilon)^{p-2}\\) below the evolving cutoff, so p>2 gives progressive high-energy suppression. A universal slope increase for every p or injection history cannot be inferred from the loss law alone."),
    sub("d.4. Interpret knot fading"),
    p("Adiabatic expansion preserves the power-law slope while reducing field and density, favouring it for AX1C and BX2. Synchrotron cooling preferentially affects the highest-energy electrons and their X-rays, favouring it for HST-1 and Knot A because their lower-frequency brightness is unchanged. These are comparative interpretations within the two mechanisms posed, not unique exclusions of all possible astrophysical causes."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2019-t3"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Tippe top"),
    p("Model a tippe top as an axisymmetric truncated sphere of radius R with a stem. Its centre of mass C is displaced from geometric centre O by αR along the axis, with 0<α<1. Before the stem touches, the floor contact A satisfies \\(\\mathbf a=\\overrightarrow{CA}=\\alpha R\\hat3-R\\hat z\\). The inertial XYZ frame has Z upward. The intermediate xyz frame rotates about Z by azimuth φ, with z=Z and symmetry axis \\(\\hat3=\\sin\\theta\\hat x+\\cos\\theta\\hat z\\). Tilting xyz about y by θ gives the 123 frame, with 2=y and \\(\\hat1=\\cos\\theta\\hat x-\\sin\\theta\\hat z\\). The axial spin angle is ψ. Principal inertias are I1=I2 and I3. The top has mass m, centre position s and sliding contact velocity vA=vx xhat+vy yhat. Kinetic friction is \\(\\mathbf F_f=-\\mu_kN\\mathbf v_A/|\\mathbf v_A|\\) when sliding, and the normal force is N. Initially the top is spun without a translational impulse. Unless asked otherwise consider θ<π/2 and no stem contact. The rotating derivative rule is \\((d\\mathbf A/dt)_{inertial}=(d\\mathbf A/dt)_{rot}+\\boldsymbol\\omega_{frame}\\times\\mathbf A\\)."),
    fig("batch7-tippe-geometry", "Original side and top views of the tippe top with sphere centre O, displaced mass centre C, contact A and tilted stem."),
    sub("a.1. Force diagram"),
    p("Find total external force and draw free-body diagrams projected onto xz and xy. Indicate vA and its relation to friction."),
    sub("a.2. Torque about centre of mass"),
    p("Find the total torque in xyz."),
    sub("a.3. No vertical contact velocity"),
    p("Use the contact condition (s+a)·zhat=0 to prove vA·zhat=0."),
    sub("a.4. Angular velocity in both frames"),
    fig("batch7-tippe-frames", "Original coordinate transformations from fixed XYZ to precessing xyz and tilted principal-axis frame 123."),
    p("Find total angular velocity in xyz and in 123 in terms of the Euler-angle derivatives."),
    sub("a.5. Energy in contact variables"),
    p("Find total energy in Euler-angle derivatives and vx,vy. Identify rotational, translational and gravitational terms."),
    sub("a.6. Vertical angular momentum rate"),
    p("Find dLz/dt about C."),
    sub("a.7. Energy transfer and dissipation"),
    p("Which forces transfer energy while the centre rises? Find the total energy-change rate and identify force and torque components relevant to the terms in A5."),
    sub("a.8. Energy sketches through inversion"),
    fig("batch7-tippe-phases", "Original five labelled phases from initial upright spin through tilt and first stem contact to inverted rest."),
    p("Sketch ET,Ug,KT,KR through phases I initially upright θ≈0, II tilting, III first stem contact θ>π/2, IV spinning inverted, V resting inverted. Axes need not have equal scales."),
    sub("a.9. Transverse inertia identity"),
    p("Show \\(\\mathbf L\\times\\hat3=k(\\boldsymbol\\omega\\times\\hat3)\\) and find k."),
    sub("a.10. Jellett integral"),
    p("Find a time-dependent vector v such that λ=L·v remains constant before stem contact, and prove its derivative vanishes."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Tippe top"),
    sub("a.1. Force diagram"),
    p("\\[\\mathbf F_{ext}=(N-mg)\\hat z+\\mathbf F_f.\\] Weight acts at C downward, N acts at A upward, and tangential friction acts at A opposite vA. The xy sketch must show opposite vA and Ff arrows. No absolute horizontal direction is prescribed."),
    sub("a.2. Torque about centre of mass"),
    p("Gravity has no torque about C. The contact torque is \\(\\boldsymbol\\tau=\\mathbf a\\times(N\\hat z+\\mathbf F_f)\\), or \\[\\tau_x=R(1-\\alpha\\cos\\theta)F_{f,y},\\quad\\tau_y=R(\\alpha\\cos\\theta-1)F_{f,x}-\\alpha RN\\sin\\theta,\\quad\\tau_z=\\alpha R\\sin\\theta F_{f,y}.\\]"),
    sub("a.3. No vertical contact velocity"),
    p("The material velocity at contact is \\(\\mathbf v_A=\\dot{\\mathbf s}+\\boldsymbol\\omega\\times\\mathbf a\\). Although a labels the instantaneous geometric contact rather than one fixed material point, \\(\\dot{\\mathbf a}=\\alpha R\\dot{\\hat3}\\) and \\(\\dot{\\hat3}=\\boldsymbol\\omega\\times\\hat3\\). The difference between adot and ω×a is horizontal. Therefore \\(\\mathbf v_A\\cdot\\hat z=(\\dot{\\mathbf s}+\\dot{\\mathbf a})\\cdot\\hat z=d[(\\mathbf s+\\mathbf a)\\cdot\\hat z]/dt=0\\)."),
    sub("a.4. Angular velocity in both frames"),
    p("Add the precession, tilt and spin: \\(\\boldsymbol\\omega=\\dot\\phi\\hat z+\\dot\\theta\\hat2+\\dot\\psi\\hat3\\). Thus \\[\\boldsymbol\\omega=\\dot\\psi\\sin\\theta\\hat x+\\dot\\theta\\hat y+(\\dot\\phi+\\dot\\psi\\cos\\theta)\\hat z\\] and \\[\\boldsymbol\\omega=-\\dot\\phi\\sin\\theta\\hat1+\\dot\\theta\\hat2+(\\dot\\psi+\\dot\\phi\\cos\\theta)\\hat3.\\]"),
    sub("a.5. Energy in contact variables"),
    p("\\(U_g=mgR(1-\\alpha\\cos\\theta)\\), \\(K_R=[I_1(\\dot\\theta^2+\\dot\\phi^2\\sin^2\\theta)+I_3(\\dot\\psi+\\dot\\phi\\cos\\theta)^2]/2\\). The centre velocity is \\[\\dot{\\mathbf s}=[v_x+R\\dot\\theta(1-\\alpha\\cos\\theta)]\\hat x+[v_y-R\\sin\\theta(\\alpha\\dot\\phi+\\dot\\psi)]\\hat y+\\alpha R\\dot\\theta\\sin\\theta\\hat z.\\] Hence KT=m|sdot|²/2 and ET=KT+KR+Ug. Squaring the displayed components gives the requested explicit expression."),
    sub("a.6. Vertical angular momentum rate"),
    p("The z axis is fixed in inertial space, so \\(\\dot L_z=\\tau_z=\\alpha R\\sin\\theta F_{f,y}\\)."),
    sub("a.7. Energy transfer and dissipation"),
    p("Contact-force power splits into centre work \\((N\\hat z+\\mathbf F_f)\\cdot\\dot{\\mathbf s}\\) and torque power \\(\\boldsymbol\\tau\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\omega\\). Their sum is contact power \\((N\\hat z+\\mathbf F_f)\\cdot\\mathbf v_A\\). Since vAz=0, \\[\\dot E_T=\\mathbf F_f\\cdot\\mathbf v_A=-\\mu_kN|\\mathbf v_A|\\le0.\\] Gravity exchanges kinetic and potential energy, with \\(\\dot U_g=mg\\alpha R\\sin\\theta\\dot\\theta\\). The normal force can do centre-of-mass work but has zero total rigid-body power at contact. Frictional Fy and its x,z torque components change vertical angular momentum and facilitate spin-to-tilt energy transfer. Friction dissipates total energy while rotational energy supplies the gravitational rise."),
    sub("a.8. Energy sketches through inversion"),
    p("ET decreases under sliding friction and approaches the final gravitational energy at rest. Ug rises during inversion and becomes constant when the stem-supported height settles. KT starts near zero, rises transiently and returns near zero in phase IV and to zero in V. KR is initially dominant, decreases overall as energy is transferred and dissipated, and vanishes in V. The source sketches it monotonically falling as a qualitative typical history, rather than a theorem for every possible trajectory. Draw all components consistently with ET=Ug+KT+KR. The spherical-contact formula for Ug ceases to apply once support transfers to the stem."),
    sub("a.9. Transverse inertia identity"),
    p("Axisymmetry gives \\(\\mathbf L=I_1\\boldsymbol\\omega+(I_3-I_1)(\\boldsymbol\\omega\\cdot\\hat3)\\hat3\\). Cross with 3hat: the second term vanishes and k=I1."),
    sub("a.10. Jellett integral"),
    p("Choose \\(\\mathbf v=\\mathbf a=\\alpha R\\hat3-R\\hat z\\), or its negative, which changes only the convention for λ. Then \\[\\dot\\lambda=\\dot{\\mathbf L}\\cdot\\mathbf a+\\alpha R\\mathbf L\\cdot(\\boldsymbol\\omega\\times\\hat3).\\] The first term vanishes because contact torque is a×F. By A9, \\(\\boldsymbol\\omega\\times\\hat3=(\\mathbf L\\times\\hat3)/I_1\\), so the second term also vanishes. It is essential to differentiate the moving vector a: torque perpendicular to a alone would not prove conservation."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2025-t1"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Precession of the Earth's axis"),
    p("Investigate the historical problem's Newtonian model of axial precession. Earth is a homogeneous oblate spheroid with equatorial radius Re, polar radius Rp, mean R=(Re+Rp)/2 and small flattening hmax=Re−Rp. Take G=6.67×10^−11 N m²/kg², R=6.371×10^6 m, ME=5.972×10^24 kg, Sun mass MS=1.989×10^30 kg at distance dSE=1.496×10^11 m, Moon mass MM=7.348×10^22 kg at dME=3.844×10^8 m, and tilt α=23.5°. Use one rotation in 24 hours and one year of 365.25 days. After part A use the empirical hmax=21 km irrespective of your estimate. For solar averaging replace the Sun by a uniform circular ring in the ecliptic plane. Set z perpendicular to that plane, with origin at Earth's centre. Near Earth all distances r,|z| are much smaller than the ring radius."),
    fig("batch7-earth-ellipsoid", "Original oblate Earth with polar radius Rp, equatorial radius Re and axial tilt alpha."),
    sub("a.1. Flattening dimensions"),
    p("In \\(h_{max}\\propto G^{-1}\\omega^\\beta M_E^\\gamma R^\\delta\\), find β,γ,δ."),
    sub("a.2. Flattening estimate"),
    p("Assume the dimensionless factor is one and evaluate hmax."),
    sub("b.1. Ring field on its axis"),
    fig("batch7-sun-ring", "Original annual solar averaging represented by spreading the Sun uniformly around its orbital ring."),
    p("Find the direction and leading magnitude of the Sun-ring gravitational field at coordinate z."),
    sub("b.2. Ring field in its plane"),
    p("Find the leading field at radial distance r in the ecliptic plane, giving direction and a derivation."),
    sub("c.1. Mass of one removed cap"),
    fig("batch7-earth-excess", "Original full equatorial-radius sphere minus two polar excess regions representing the oblate Earth."),
    p("Imagine the spheroid as a full sphere of radius Re minus two equal excess polar regions. Find the mass m of one removed region in hmax,ME,Rp."),
    sub("c.2. Solar torque"),
    p("The torque on the removed regions equals that on two point masses 2m/5 at the endpoints of the polar diameter. Find the torque magnitude on Earth to first order in hmax/R, and state its sense."),
    sub("d.1. Solar-only precession period"),
    p("Derive the precession period T1 in MS,dSE,ω,hmax,R,α."),
    sub("d.2. Solar-only numerical period"),
    p("Evaluate T1 in years using hmax=21 km."),
    sub("e.1. Lunar correction factor"),
    p("Now approximate the Moon's orbit as a circle in the ecliptic plane. Find T2/T1 after adding its averaged torque."),
    sub("e.2. Combined numerical period"),
    p("Evaluate T2 in years."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Precession of the Earth's axis"),
    sub("a.1. Flattening dimensions"),
    p("Using [G]=L³M^−1T^−2, dimensional balance gives time exponent 2−β=0, mass exponent 1+γ=0 and length exponent δ−3=1. Thus β=2, γ=−1, δ=4."),
    sub("a.2. Flattening estimate"),
    p("\\(\\omega=2\\pi/86400=7.27\\times10^{-5}\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}\\). Therefore \\(h_{max}=\\omega^2R^4/(GM_E)\\simeq21.9\\,\\mathrm{km}\\)."),
    sub("b.1. Ring field on its axis"),
    p("The exact potential is \\(\\Phi(z)=-GM_S/\\sqrt{d_{SE}^2+z^2}\\). Thus \\(g_z=-GM_Sz/(d_{SE}^2+z^2)^{3/2}\\simeq-GM_Sz/d_{SE}^3\\). It points toward the ecliptic plane for either sign of z."),
    sub("b.2. Ring field in its plane"),
    p("Symmetry and the mass-free divergence condition near the centre give \\(2g_r/r+\\partial_zg_z=0\\), so \\[g_r=GM_Sr/(2d_{SE}^3),\\] radially outwards. Alternatively expand the ring potential through r²: \\(\\Phi(r)=-GM_S/d_{SE}-GM_Sr^2/(4d_{SE}^3)\\), then differentiate. The axial restoring field and in-plane outward field have opposite signs."),
    sub("c.1. Mass of one removed cap"),
    p("The spheroid volume is \\(4\\pi R_e^2R_p/3\\). Half the difference from a sphere is \\(2\\pi R_e^2h_{max}/3\\). Multiply by density \\(3M_E/(4\\pi R_e^2R_p)\\) to obtain \\(m=M_Eh_{max}/(2R_p)\\)."),
    sub("c.2. Solar torque"),
    p("A complete sphere has zero torque, so Earth's torque is opposite that on the removed masses. Both the vertical restoring and radial outward fields contribute. Their combined torque magnitude is \\(6GmM_SR^2\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\alpha/(5d_{SE}^3)\\). Substitute m≈MEhmax/(2R): \\[|\\tau|=\\frac{3GM_EM_SR h_{max}}{5d_{SE}^3}\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\alpha.\\] If the north axis lies in +xz, this torque is along −y, giving retrograde precession."),
    sub("d.1. Solar-only precession period"),
    p("To leading order the spin angular momentum is \\(L=(2/5)M_ER^2\\omega\\). The magnitude changes slowly compared with direction. Precession gives \\(|\\dot{\\mathbf L}|=\\Omega_1L\\sin\\alpha=|\\tau|\\). Hence \\[T_1=\\frac{2\\pi}{\\Omega_1}=\\frac{4\\pi d_{SE}^3R\\omega}{3GM_Sh_{max}\\cos\\alpha}.\\]"),
    sub("d.2. Solar-only numerical period"),
    p("The model gives \\(T_1\\simeq8.06\\times10^4\\) years. This is larger than the observed precession period because the Moon has not yet been included."),
    sub("e.1. Lunar correction factor"),
    p("The same ring calculation applies and torque coefficients add as M/d³. Thus \\[\\frac{T_2}{T_1}=\\frac{M_S/d_{SE}^3}{M_S/d_{SE}^3+M_M/d_{ME}^3}.\\] The lunar torque makes precession faster and the period shorter."),
    sub("e.2. Combined numerical period"),
    p("The ratio is approximately 0.315. Multiplying the solar-only period gives \\(T_2\\simeq2.54\\times10^4\\) years in this homogeneous-Earth, coplanar-circular-orbit model."),
  ],
};
